{
  "5ec2cd6a-51e3-4a3d-8ebc-b35b9a060dc9": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "My favourite hobby is listen music. I like to do this every day. I have a smartphone and headphones. I open a music app and then I choose songs. Sometime I sing along, but I am not good singer. I listen to different kind of music, like pop and also some rock. I enjoy listen music because it makes me feel very happy. When I am feeling sad, music can make me feel better. It is good for relaxing after a long day. I like the sound and the beat of the music. It is very fun for me.",
    "mid": "My favourite hobby is reading book. I like read many kinds of books, like adventure novels and some history books. I often read them in the evening, maybe before I go to sleep. When I am reading, I feel very relax. Books can take me to different world and I can learn new thing from them. It help me forget about my daily stress and just enjoy a good story. I enjoy it a lot because it make me calm and also clever.",
    "high": "My favourite pastime is undoubtedly reading. I frequently immerse myself in various genres, from gripping crime thrillers to historical fiction, and occasionally delve into non-fiction to broaden my knowledge. I typically enjoy this activity during quiet evenings at home or when commuting, finding solace in turning pages. Reading offers a wonderful escape from daily pressures, allowing me to explore different worlds and perspectives. It not only helps me relax and de-stress but also enriches my understanding of complex ideas and cultures, making it an invaluable part of my routine."
  },
  "fb482fe0-ad48-40b2-a576-b115943c0ea2": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "I want to write about my daily routine. My life is quite normal. A daily routine is very important for many people. It help to make life simple and organized. So I will tell you about what I do every day, from the morning until night.\n\nMy daily routine is simple. Every morning I wake up at 7 o'clock. **First**, I go to bathroom to wash my face. Then I drink coffee and eat bread for breakfast. **After that**, I go to my job at the small shop. I work from nine morning to five evening. Is long day. When I finish work, I go home on the bus. In the evening, I cook dinner for my family. We eat together. **Finally**, I watch some TV and listen music before I sleep around 10:30 o'clock. It is very same every day.\n\nThis is my normal daily life from Monday to Friday. It is not very exciting but it is how I live every week. On the weekend I do different things with my family and friends. But for the working days, this routine is very important for me and my family.",
    "mid": "My daily routine is mostly same. **First**, I wake up early at around 6:30 AM, and I always drink a cup of coffee. **Then**, I quickly prepare breakfast, usually just toast and tea. **After that**, I leave my home for work. I work as an assistant in a office. I come back home late afternoon, around 5:30. **Finally**, I make dinner and relax by watching some television programs. Sometime I also read a book before I go to bed at about 10:00 PM.",
    "high": "My typical day begins quite early. **First**, I wake up around 6:30 AM, have breakfast, and prepare for my day. **Then**, I spend most of my morning and early afternoon either studying for my IELTS exam or attending online lectures for my university course, which involves reading and practising writing. **After that**, I usually take a break to exercise, perhaps a short run, to clear my mind. My evening routine involves cooking dinner and relaxing, watching a documentary or reading a novel. **Finally**, I aim to be in bed by 10:30 PM to ensure adequate rest."
  },
  "64d422b4-2bd7-47ad-a351-1e2784f3bd42": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "My favourite season is summer because I like very much hot weather. In summer, the sun shine all day and I can go outside with my friends. We play football in the park, and sometimes we go to a beach for swim. Also, the days are very long, so I do not need to come home when it is still light. I also like to eat ice cream many times. Summer is best time for me and my family to relax and have fun.",
    "mid": "My favourite season is definitely spring. I like it very much because the weather is always very nice, not too cold like winter and not too hot like summer, so it is comfortable. It is a good time for go outside and do many activities. Also, many beautiful flowers start to open everywhere and new trees grow green leaves after winter. You can see many baby animals born, like small birds or lambs, which make me happy. This make nature feel fresh and new again. So, spring is a very good time of year for everyone to enjoy.",
    "high": "My favourite season is undoubtedly autumn. I am captivated by the breathtaking transformation of nature, as leaves change from vivid green to brilliant shades of red, orange, and gold, creating truly stunning landscapes. The mild weather during this period is also ideal, offering cool, crisp air perfect for long walks without the extreme heat of summer or the biting cold of winter. Moreover, autumn evokes a sense of comfort and preparation, often associated with warm beverages and the lead-up to festive holidays. For these reasons, it is the most enchanting time of the year."
  },
  "e8c39b6f-8076-4a3b-b6de-c784a8982dcb": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Dear Sarah,\n\nI hope you are doing good. I want to invite you to my birthday party! I am very excited for it. It will be a nice evening with food and music.\n\nMy party is on Saturday, 15th July. It will be at my house, 123 Main Street. We are starting the party at 7 PM. Please come and join to celebrate with me.\n\nI really hope you can come. It would be very nice if you are there. We will have a good time together.\n\nSee you then,\nAhmed",
    "mid": "Dear Sarah,\n\nI want to invite you to my birthday party! I hope you can come. It is very important for me.\n\nMy party will be on Saturday, 20th July. It start at 7 PM in the evening. We are having it at my home, 123 Main Street. There will be good music and some nice food. I am very happy to celebrate with all my friend.\n\nPlease tell me if you can come by next week, so I know how many peoples there will be.\n\nSee you soon,\n[Your Name]",
    "high": "Dear Alex,\n\nI'm so excited to invite you to my birthday party! I'd absolutely love for you to come and celebrate with me.\n\nThe party is scheduled for **Saturday, 19th August**, starting at **7:30 PM**. We'll be having it at **my apartment** – there'll be plenty of food, music, and good company guaranteed!\n\nPlease let me know if you can make it by next week so I can finalise the arrangements. I'm really looking forward to seeing you there!\n\nBest,\nChris"
  },
  "ec3eeacb-c2f8-495b-90c3-433041d41316": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Working from home is a way for job that many peoples choose today. This new system have become more popular.\n\nOn one hand, working at home have many good things. Firstly, peoples no need travel to office every day. This save time and money for them, and also reduce stress. They can wear comfortable clothes and work from their house. In addition, it is possible to spend more time with family, for example, eat lunch with children. This make life more balance and happy.\n\nHowever, there are also some bad points. For example, it can be very lonely. Peoples not see colleagues every day, so they feel alone sometimes. Also, it is hard to separate work and home life. The bed is near the computer, so maybe peoples want to sleep or watch TV. It is difficult to focus sometimes because distractions in home like family members.\n\nOverall, working from home has both good and bad parts. It offer flexibility but also bring some challenges like loneliness and focus problems. Many companies now think about this system for their staff.",
    "mid": "Working from home has become very popular and it offers both good and bad things. On one hand, a major advantage is that people save a lot of time and money because they do not need to travel to the office every day. This mean more time for family or personal hobbies. In addition, many finds the home environment more comfortable and less stressful, which can increase productivity for some tasks.\n\nHowever, there are also some clear disadvantages to this setup. Working alone at home can lead to feelings of isolation and a lack of social interaction with colleagues, which is important for many people. Furthermore, it can be difficult to separate work life from personal life, as the office is always at home. Distractions like household chores or family members can also make concentration difficult for some employees. Overall, while flexible work brings convenience, it also needs good self-discipline and clear boundaries.",
    "high": "Working from home has become increasingly common, offering both compelling benefits and considerable challenges for employees. **On the one hand**, one of the most significant advantages is the enhanced flexibility it provides, allowing individuals to manage their schedules more effectively and often work in a more comfortable environment. **In addition**, it eliminates daily commutes, resulting in significant time and cost savings, which can positively impact personal well-being and productivity. **However**, this setup is not without its drawbacks. Many individuals experience a sense of isolation due to reduced social interaction with colleagues, potentially impacting team cohesion and mental health. **Furthermore**, distinguishing between professional and personal life can become difficult, leading to a blurring of boundaries as the home environment doubles as an office. This can result in employees working longer hours or struggling to switch off, ultimately diminishing their work-life balance. **Overall**, while remote work offers appealing autonomy, it demands careful management to mitigate its inherent challenges."
  },
  "9890be01-cd54-4317-9d6d-411b5858e9cb": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "In modern day world, technology is very big part of our life. Many people, especial young generation, use technology all the time. They have smartphone, tablet, and computer. These tools are everywhere now. In the past, young people communicate by meeting face-to-face or using a house phone. But now, it is very different. Young people use social media applications such as WhatsApp, Instagram, and TikTok to talk to their friends and family. They send message fast, share picture, and make video call. This way of talking is much faster and easier for them. However, it also mean they not always meet in person. This essay will look at the various ways technology has really changed how young people communicate with other people in today society.",
    "mid": "In today fast-paced world, technology has become a very important part of everyone life, especially for young people. It is no longer surprised that most teenagers and young adults are constantly connected to their digital devices. Before, young people mainly communicated by meeting in person or using phone calls on landlines. However, this old ways of communication has dramatically changed. The rise of smartphones, social media apps like Instagram and TikTok, and instant messaging services mean that young people now communicate very different. These digital tools allow them to connect with friends and family anytime and anywhere. Therefore, this essay will discuss how these technological advancements have significantly impact the way young people interact and exchange information, bringing both positive and negative influences on their social lives.",
    "high": "The digital revolution has undeniably transformed nearly every facet of modern life, with communication standing out as one of the most profoundly impacted areas. For younger generations, who have grown up in an era defined by smartphones and high-speed internet, the concept of interaction has been fundamentally reshaped. Gone are the days when face-to-face conversations or landline calls were the primary means of connecting; instead, social media platforms, instant messaging applications, and video calls have become ubiquitous, forming the very fabric of their social existence. This paradigm shift has not only altered *how* young people connect but also *what* they communicate and *with whom*. This essay will explore the extensive impact of technology on youth communication, delving into both the undeniable benefits of increased connectivity and the significant challenges posed by this new digital landscape."
  },
  "3d47f523-11e1-42f1-b623-a2ed3b138127": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "AI is big now in world. Some people say AI will take human creativity work, like artist and music. I think this is partly true, but human creativity is very different from machine.\n\nFirst, AI can make many things that look creative. Computer can make art pictures very fast, like painting. It can write music notes for songs. And AI also can write simple story like human do. So, if AI do this good, maybe human not need to make creative things. This is worry for many people, because they think AI is faster and cheaper. My friend say AI good at copy old style, but not new.\n\nBut, human creativity is from brain and real feeling. AI is only machine. It not have real feeling or special idea in its head. Human make very new idea, very special idea. AI only use data what it learn from internet. So, real new thing come from human, not computer. Because human have imagination from heart and soul, machine not have soul. This is important for real invention and art.\n\nIn conclusion, AI can make creative things, yes, and it is clever. But human creativity is special because it come from human mind and feeling deep inside. So I not agree fully that AI will replace everything. Human creativity will always be important for new things and unique ideas.",
    "mid": "Many people worry that artificial intelligence (AI) will take over creative jobs from humans. I agree partially that AI can help, but I think it will not replace human creativity completely, because human have unique things that machines cannot do.\n\nFirstly, creativity from humans come from their feelings and life experiences. For example, an artist can draw after a sad time, or a writer can write story about his love. AI is not having feelings or own experiences. It just learn from data that people feed into it. So, it cannot make real original or emotional art like human can.\n\nSecond, AI can be a good tool to help human creativity. For example, a graphic designer might use AI to get many ideas fast for a logo. But the human designer always chooses the best idea and add their own special touch. Therefore, AI make work faster, but the real creative thinking and new ideas always come from people's minds.\n\nTo conclude, I believe AI will not replace human creativity. This is because real creativity needs human emotions and personal experiences, and AI is better as a helpful tool for humans.",
    "high": "Recent advancements in artificial intelligence have led some to predict its potential to usurp human creative endeavors. While AI undeniably possesses remarkable capabilities in generating novel content, I largely disagree with the notion that it will fundamentally replace human creativity.\n\nThe essence of human creativity lies in its connection to consciousness, emotion, and subjective experience. Artists, writers, and musicians draw inspiration from their unique understanding of the world, their joys, sorrows, and abstract thoughts, leading to truly original expressions. Conversely, AI operates by algorithms, processing vast datasets to identify patterns and generate outputs that mimic human work. Consequently, its \"creativity\" is derivative, a sophisticated recombination of existing information, rather than an authentic spark of imagination.\n\nIndeed, AI serves as an immensely powerful tool for augmenting human creative processes. It can automate repetitive tasks, generate countless variations, or even propose novel ideas based on learned styles. For instance, AI can compose music in a specific genre or design countless logo options. However, the initial conceptualisation, the emotional depth, and the *why* behind a creative piece remain firmly within the human domain. It lacks the intentionality and emotional resonance that defines truly groundbreaking human artistic output.\n\nIn conclusion, while artificial intelligence offers exciting possibilities as a creative assistant, its fundamental reliance on algorithms and data prevents it from replicating the profound, emotionally driven, and inherently human wellspring of creativity. Therefore, rather than replacing us, AI is poised to become a sophisticated collaborator, enhancing rather than diminishing human imaginative capacity."
  },
  "e9dcf8fb-0cef-4a25-91c7-4b4b7f10d092": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Culture globalisation is when many country culture mix together. This is a big thing in today world. It have both good consequence and bad consequence for people. I will discuss both side now.\n\nFirst, there are some good points. When culture mix, people can learn new thing. For example, we can try different food from other country, like pizza or sushi. Also, we can listen to different music or watch movies from around the world. This can make world more interesting and understand each other more. It is good for new idea.\n\nHowever, there also bad points. A big problem is that own culture can lost. If all culture become same, small culture will disappear. This mean tradition and old way of life will go away. People might forget their history. It also make country lose its unique identity. Everyone become same and this is not good for diversity.\n\nI think is both good and bad. It is good to learn from others, but it is important to keep own culture also. We must not forget where we come from. So, globalisation culture need balance. We need keep our own traditions strong when we learn new things.",
    "mid": "Globalisation of culture is a topic that many people talk about. It means when cultures from different countries mix together. This can have good things and bad things happen. This essay will look at both sides.\n\nFirstly, a good thing about cultural globalisation is that people can learn about other cultures. For example, we can eat food from other countries like pizza or sushi. This makes our life more interesting. Also, it can help people understand each other better and make peace. When we know about other cultures, we are not scared of them.\n\nOn the other hand, there are bad consequences. One problem is that some cultures might lose their own unique identity. For instance, many young people listen to pop music from America, and they do not listen to their traditional music. So, their own culture can be forgotten. Also, bigger cultures can become too strong and make smaller cultures disappear. This is a sad thing for the world.\n\nIn conclusion, cultural globalisation has both positive and negative side. It help people to learn and share new things, but it also risk to lose traditional cultures. My opinion is that we should try to keep our own cultures while we also enjoy new things from other countries.",
    "high": "The growing interconnectedness of the world has led to significant cultural integration, a phenomenon often termed globalisation. This essay will explore both the positive and negative consequences of this trend before presenting my perspective.\n\nOn the one hand, cultural globalisation fosters greater understanding and tolerance between diverse populations. Through exposure to different traditions, cuisines, and art forms, individuals can develop a broader worldview, enriching their own lives. For instance, the popularity of international music genres or foreign films allows for a shared appreciation that can bridge geographical divides and challenge stereotypes.\n\nConversely, a major concern is the potential erosion of unique local identities. As dominant cultures, often Western, spread globally through media and commerce, there is a risk that traditional customs, languages, and art forms from smaller communities could be marginalized or even lost entirely. This homogenization reduces the rich tapestry of human diversity and can lead to a sense of cultural alienation among those whose heritage is overlooked.\n\nIn conclusion, while cultural globalisation undoubtedly offers opportunities for mutual appreciation and the sharing of diverse experiences, it simultaneously poses a serious threat to the preservation of distinct cultural heritage. In my opinion, it is crucial to celebrate global exchange while actively supporting efforts to protect and promote local traditions to maintain the world's cultural richness."
  },
  "feec2451-6485-4f09-b5ff-852d7ae8b3a9": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Many people think public transport should be free. This is a big idea for many city today. I will discuss about good and bad things for this.\n\nOne good point is for people who no have much money. They can go work or go shop easy. This help them much. Also, it mean less cars on road. When less cars, the air is more clean. Not much bad smoke come out. City place can be more quiet too. Many people may walk or use bike instead of drive car. This good for environment.\n\nBut there are also bad points. Who will pay for the bus and train? Government need to use tax money. This mean every people pay more tax money maybe. If many people use free transport, the buses will be very full. Hard to find seat. Drivers also need to be pay. And the bus need repair sometimes. If no money come, the buses get broken and not work good. Many problem with maintain.\n\nIn my mind, free transport sound good for environment and for people who poor. But it is very hard to make happen because money is big problem. Many things must solve before public transport can be free for all.",
    "mid": "Some people believe that public transport should be free for everyone. This idea have both good points and bad points.\n\nOne main advantage is it can reduce traffic on the road. If buses and trains are free, more peoples will leave their cars at home. This mean less pollution in the cities and fewer traffic jams. Also, it help people who do not have much money. They can travel to work or school without paying, which is a big saving for them. This can improve their quality of life.\n\nHowever, there are also some disadvantages. The biggest problem is how to pay for this. If no one pay for tickets, the government must find the money from somewhere, perhaps from taxes. This could make taxes higher for everyone. Another issue is that free transport might become very crowded. Peoples might use it for very short journeys, or just for fun, which make it difficult for serious commuters. Also, the quality of services might go down if there is not enough money for maintenance or new vehicles.\n\nIn conclusion, making public transport free have good reasons like less traffic and helping poor people. But it also creates challenges about funding and overcrowding. It is a complicated idea.",
    "high": "Making public transport free has gained attention, with proponents highlighting benefits and critics pointing out financial hurdles. This essay will discuss both advantages and disadvantages of this idea.\n\nA key advantage is the potential to drastically reduce private car usage, alleviating traffic congestion and lowering carbon emissions, thus contributing to environmental sustainability. Moreover, free transport would make commuting more accessible for lower-income individuals, potentially boosting local economies and fostering greater urban mobility. This could also reduce household expenditure for many, freeing up income for other uses.\n\nConversely, a major disadvantage concerns funding. Without fares, governments must cover operational costs and maintenance via increased taxation, a significant fiscal burden on taxpayers. This might also lead to a decline in service quality due to less investment in infrastructure upgrades or staff, as funds may be stretched thin. Furthermore, free services could attract overcrowding, making journeys unpleasant and unsafe during peak hours, and potentially deterring existing paying passengers.\n\nIn conclusion, while free public transport offers clear benefits like environmental improvement and social equity, the substantial financial implications and potential service deterioration pose significant challenges. Policymakers must carefully weigh these pros and cons."
  },
  "57a97a09-7e0f-48f5-8988-e569934af567": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Many young people choosing to study in abroad country. This is a very popular trend today for young person. They go to different country for study many subject.\n\nThere are some reasons for this trend. First, many young student want a better education. They think universities in other country like USA or UK are more good for them. Also, a big reason is to learn English language. English is a important language for work and travel. So, young people hope to improve their English skills by living in English speaking country. This help them to get a better job in the future.\n\nThis trend has both good and bad effects. One good effect is that young people can learn many new things. They see new culture, eat new food and meet different peoples. This make them more independent person. But also, there are some bad effects. Study abroad is very expensive for family. Young people can feel lonely and miss their family and friend from home. It is sometime difficult to adapt to a new country life and different habits.\n\nIn summary, young people choosing to study abroad for good education and to learn English language. This trend have both positive and negative results for them and their family.",
    "mid": "It is becoming popular for young people to choose study in other countries. This essay will explain the reasons why they do this and also discuss the possible effects of this trend.\n\nOne main reason for this trend is that many students look for a higher quality education. Some foreign universities are world famous and offer special courses that are not available in their home country. Also, young people want to experience new cultures and improve their English language skills. They believe that international experience can help them get better jobs after finishing their studies.\n\nThis trend has several effects. On the positive side, students become more independent and develop a global mindset. They learn to adapt to new situations and meet people from different backgrounds. However, there are also negative effects. Many young people face challenges like homesickness and financial difficulties because studying abroad can be very expensive. Sometimes it is hard for them to get used to the new culture and make friends.\n\nIn conclusion, young people go abroad for better education and to experience new cultures. This decision leads to personal development but can also bring problems like loneliness and financial stress.",
    "high": "The phenomenon of young individuals opting to pursue their education in foreign countries has become increasingly prevalent globally.\n\nThere are several compelling reasons behind this growing trend. Firstly, many international institutions offer a higher quality of education or specialized courses not available domestically, attracting ambitious students. Secondly, a foreign degree is often perceived to significantly enhance future career prospects, opening doors to lucrative global employment markets and networking opportunities. Furthermore, the desire for personal development, independence, and profound cultural immersion also motivates young people to seek experiences abroad.\n\nThe effects of this trend are multifaceted. On the positive side, studying abroad significantly fosters independence, critical thinking, and invaluable cross-cultural communication skills, all essential in today's interconnected world. It also broadens perspectives, promotes global understanding, and builds resilience. However, potential drawbacks include significant financial costs, the challenge of culture shock, and difficulties in readapting to one's home country after an extended period away.\n\nIn conclusion, while driven by academic and personal ambitions, this trend ultimately shapes individuals into more globally aware and skilled citizens."
  },
  "ab975d70-6b96-4773-8a71-2673081bc920": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Some people say when a country has more money, it means it is getting good. They think economic grow is important. When there is more money, people can get jobs and buy many things. This make life easy and new shops and buildings can build. So, for some, more money is the only way a country is strong and progress.\n\nBut other people think different. They say happy people and clean nature is more important than money. If people are sick because air is bad or water is not clean, then money not help much. Also, if people are sad or stress, even with much money, life is not good. So, for them, clean environment and good life for people is the real sign of a country go forward.\n\nI think both are important but happy people and clean place is more important. What is money if people are sick from pollution or they are not happy? A country need some money but also need to care about people health and the world around us. So, for me, well-being and clean environment is the best for a nation.",
    "mid": "Some people believe that only economic growth show if a country is developing well. However, others think that the well-being of people and good environment is more important. This essay will look at both ideas and share my opinion.\n\nFirstly, it is easy to understand why economic growth is seen important. When a country's economy grows, there are often more jobs and people can get higher salary. This makes it possible for families to have a better life and buy things they need. Also, governments with strong economy can invest in schools and hospitals, which makes society better.\n\nHowever, money is not only thing. If people is rich but very stressed, or if the air and water is dirty, then it is not true progress. For example, some countries grow fast but their citizens are not happy. Also, making much money can damage our planet, like cutting down forests. These things can make big problem for future, so well-being and nature is very important.\n\nTo conclude, both economic growth and other things like people happiness and healthy environment are important. I believe that people well-being and protecting nature is more critical because without this, a country cannot be truly good in a long run. A balance is necessary for nation progress.",
    "high": "The debate surrounding the true measure of a nation's progress is multifaceted. Some proponents assert that economic growth, often quantified by GDP, is the most reliable indicator, arguing that a robust economy provides the tangible resources necessary for societal advancement. Increased wealth can lead to better infrastructure, more employment opportunities, and improved public services like healthcare and education, thereby elevating living standards for its citizens.\n\nConversely, many believe that relying solely on economic metrics overlooks crucial aspects of human development and long-term sustainability. They contend that a nation's prosperity should also be measured by its citizens' well-being, encompassing happiness, mental health, and social equity, alongside the health of its environment. Unchecked economic pursuit often leads to detrimental consequences such as pollution, resource depletion, and increased social inequalities, ultimately undermining the quality of life and future prospects.\n\nIn my opinion, a truly progressive nation must adopt a holistic approach. While economic prosperity provides a foundational base, it should not come at the expense of human welfare or planetary health. A balanced strategy that integrates sustainable development, social equity, and environmental protection alongside economic growth offers a more comprehensive and ultimately more reliable measure of national success."
  },
  "38441093-eb6e-436d-8c1f-62ea5af462de": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Today, many new technology things happen very fast. Some people think that the old school papers, like degrees from university, are not important so much anymore. I agree with this idea a little.\n\nFirst, many jobs today need different skills. For example, if you want to work with computers or on the internet, companies want to see what you can actually do. You can learn these computer skills online or by practicing a lot. You do not always need to go to university for many years. Many people get good jobs because they have practical skills, not just a paper. My friend, she learn web design by herself, and now she work for a small company. She did not go to university for this.\n\nAlso, university is very expensive and take a long time. Some people can start working earlier and get real experience. They can learn more practical things faster this way. Then, they have a good job and do not have big debt from school. So, the value of only having a paper from school is maybe less now.\n\nHowever, some jobs, like a doctor or a lawyer, still need university for many years. These are very special jobs. But for many other jobs, I think practical skills and experience are more important now than just old degrees. So, traditional qualifications are losing some value in this fast changing world.",
    "mid": "In today rapid technological world, some people think that traditional degrees from universities are not as useful as before. I partly agree with this idea, because new skills are needed, but I also think that qualifications still have importance.\n\nOn one hand, technology is changing jobs very fast. Many new jobs need different skills, like coding or digital marketing, which traditional degrees sometimes do not teach in deep. People can learn these skills online or from short courses, making them ready for work quicker than a long university degree. For example, some successful IT professionals do not have a university degree but they have strong technical skills. This show that practical experience can be more important for some industries.\n\nHowever, it is wrong to say academic qualifications lost all their value. A university degree still gives a strong foundation of knowledge and critical thinking skills. Many employers still look for candidates with a degree because it show dedication and a certain level of understanding. For example, doctors or engineers must have specific academic qualifications to practice their job safety and correctly. These fields always need traditional education.\n\nSo, while technology brings new ways to learn and work, traditional academic qualifications still hold value for many jobs and provide important background knowledge. I think the value is not lost, but it is changing for what types of jobs.",
    "high": "While it is undeniable that rapid technological advancements have transformed the job market, asserting that traditional academic qualifications are entirely losing their value is an oversimplification. I partially disagree with this statement, believing their importance has shifted rather than diminished.\n\nIt is true that many roles now prioritise immediate practical skills, often gained through online courses or vocational training, over a generic university degree. Tech giants frequently recruit based on demonstrable abilities and portfolios, suggesting a move away from solely relying on academic transcripts. This reflects a demand for agile learning and specific, up-to-date competencies.\n\nHowever, this perspective overlooks the foundational understanding and critical thinking abilities fostered by traditional education. Degrees in fields like engineering, medicine, or law remain indispensable, providing a rigorous theoretical base and analytical frameworks that cannot be easily replicated by short courses. Furthermore, universities cultivate transferable skills such as research, problem-solving, and sophisticated communication, which are crucial for adapting to future, as yet unknown, technological changes. Therefore, while their exclusive dominance might be waning, academic qualifications still offer a significant and enduring value, particularly for roles requiring deep conceptual knowledge and adaptability."
  },
  "e617e051-c0fd-4e00-a819-f738d2e4e414": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, my hometown is uh... call Agra. It is locate in north India. It is a very big city, uh, very famous for the Taj Mahal. Many tourist come here, uh, every year. It is very busy city, many people, very noisy. And sometimes it is very hot. But it also have many old building and some new shop. What I like is uh... the food. Street food is very good. And uh, my all family live there. So I like to be with my family and my friend. But sometimes it is many traffic, very difficult to move. But I like my home, my city.",
    "mid": "My hometown is called Guanmiao. It is located in the southern part of my country. It is not a big city, actually it is more like a town, a small town.\n\nWhat it is like? Well, it's quite a quiet place. We don't have many tall building or very big shopping mall. But it has many traditional market and some local shop. There is a small river run through the town, and many green spaces around. The people there is usually very friendly, you know, neighbours helping neighbours.\n\nWhat I like about it is the peaceful atmosphere. It is not too busy, so I can relax. And also, I like the sense of community. Everyone know each other, so it feel very safe. It's a very good place for raise a family.",
    "high": "Well, my hometown is called Guangzhou, and it’s a really large, vibrant city located in the south of China, specifically in Guangdong province. It's often referred to as a \"first-tier\" city.\n\nWhat it's like is quite dynamic; it’s a huge economic hub with a very fast pace of life, full of skyscrapers and busy streets. However, it also has a rich history, with beautiful ancient temples and traditional gardens nestled amongst the modern architecture. It’s famous for its delicious Cantonese cuisine, which is a major part of the culture there.\n\nWhat I really like about Guangzhou is the food scene – you can find incredible dishes everywhere, from street food to fine dining. I also appreciate the subtropical climate, which means it’s warm for most of the year. There’s a constant energy about the place that makes it very exciting to live in."
  },
  "b0fc2ce7-eb02-4e20-bb2b-8c32ab74ccf4": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, my typical day. I wake up, hmm, usually six o'clock, six thirty. In morning, I eat breakfast, just some bread and tea. Then I go to work. My job is clean office, you know. It finish at one o'clock. So, afternoon, I am home. I cook food for my family, my children. We eat lunch. After that, I clean house a little bit. Evening, we watch TV. My children play sometime. I talk my husband. Then we go to bed. I like evening most. Because my family is all together. We can relax and no work, you know? It's a happy time.",
    "mid": "Okay, so for me, a typical day start early. I usually wake up around 7 o'clock in the morning. After that, I quickly get up and have my breakfast, usually some bread and tea. Then I prepare myself for work.\n\nIn the morning, I go to my office and work for few hours. For the afternoon, I continue work after a quick lunch break with my colleagues. When the evening come, I finish work and go back home. I cook my dinner, or sometimes my family cook. After dinner, I just relax and maybe watch some TV. I go to bed around 11.\n\nI enjoy the evening most because it's time for relax. No more stress from work, and I can be myself at home.",
    "high": "Typically, I wake up around 7 AM on weekdays. My mornings are usually quite busy; I quickly get ready, have a light breakfast, and then commute to my office. I start working shortly after, focusing on my tasks until lunchtime. The afternoon involves more work, often attending virtual meetings or collaborating with colleagues. I usually finish my main duties by 5:30 PM. In the evenings, I head home, prepare a simple dinner, and then I like to unwind. This might involve reading a book, watching a TV series, or chatting with my family. I aim to be in bed by 10:30 PM. The part of the day I enjoy most is definitely the evening, as it’s a time for relaxation and personal pursuits after a demanding day."
  },
  "bc747a62-417f-4aba-be87-4854ce654bcb": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "I remember one trip. It was to the beach. This beach is not far from my home, like maybe one hour drive. I go with my family. My mom, my dad, and my little sister. We go there for one day. We bring some food, like sandwiches and juice.\n\nWhen we arrived, the weather was sunny. Very good. My sister and me, we played in the sand. We build a castle, but it was not very good. Then, we go to swim in the sea. The water was a little bit cold but it was fun. My dad, he like to read book, so he sit on the sand.\n\nIt was memorable because it was my first time to see the ocean. And I play with my sister and my family, so it was very happy day for me. We don't go often on trip, so this one was special.",
    "mid": "Okay, I want to talk about a memorable trip. I went to Nha Trang, a city in Vietnam, about five years ago. I was still in high school then.\n\nI went with my family; my parents and younger sister. It was our summer holiday.\n\nWe did many things. We swam in the clear, warm sea. We ate delicious fresh seafood daily. We also visited a local market.\n\nIt was memorable because it was my first time to see the ocean. I was so excited to see the big water and sand! Plus, it was a good time with my family, relaxing and laughing together. A very special trip for me.",
    "high": "One truly memorable trip I've taken was a hiking adventure in the Scottish Highlands a few years ago. I went with two of my closest university friends, and it was actually our first big trip together after graduating, so that made it special from the start.\n\nWe spent about ten days there, primarily hiking through incredible landscapes – think vast glens, towering mountains, and serene lochs. We also explored a few charming, remote villages, tried local food, and even spotted some red deer.\n\nIt was memorable for several reasons. Firstly, the sheer beauty of the rugged scenery was breathtaking; every turn brought a new, stunning vista. Secondly, the physical challenge of some of the hikes, especially climbing Ben Nevis, was immensely rewarding. And finally, sharing those intense experiences and creating lasting memories with my best friends made it truly unforgettable. It really cemented our friendship."
  },
  "53276d9f-4ab1-441b-aa34-e0ecc4745831": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I want to learn a skill... is cooking. Yes, I want to learn cooking. Because... uhm... I don't cook now. My mother cook always. So, I want to learn for myself. It's very good.\n\nWhy I want to learn? Because I want to eat my own food. Sometimes I eat outside, but my home food is better, I think. And healthy. My mother sometimes busy, so I want to help her also.\n\nHow I learn? I think I will ask my mother. She is good cook. So she can teach me. Maybe I watch some videos also, from YouTube. And I will try in the kitchen. Practice little bit.\n\nIt will benefit me very much. I can cook for myself when I am alone. And I can cook for my family sometimes. It is a good skill for future, when I have my own home. Yes.",
    "mid": "A skill I really want to learn is cooking. I mean, how to make different dishes, not just simple ones.\n\nI want to learn it because right now I mostly eat outside. This is very expensive and not always healthy, you know? I want to cook healthy food for myself.\n\nI think I would learn by watching videos on YouTube; they have many easy tutorials. Also, I would ask my mother to teach me some simple recipes, like a basic curry or pasta. I'd start with easy ones.\n\nIt would benefit me a lot because it saves money, and I can eat healthier. It’s also a very useful skill for my life, and I can cook for friends.",
    "high": "I’d really love to learn Spanish fluently. I've always been fascinated by Latin American cultures and travel, and I feel understanding the language would truly open so many doors – for personal enrichment, immersive travel, and deeper communication with friends who are native speakers.\n\nTo learn it, I'd start with structured online courses and daily practice on an app like Duolingo. Crucially, I'd then find a language exchange partner or tutor for regular conversational practice, as speaking is paramount.\n\nThe main benefit would be incredible personal enrichment and hugely improved travel experiences. Imagine navigating bustling markets or chatting authentically with locals! Professionally, it could also become a significant asset in my increasingly globalized field."
  },
  "bbd02506-1e1f-4927-805d-c5094001981e": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Uh, yes, I think is, um, serious concern. Because society is, uh, very modern now. So many new things, you know, and people forget old skills. Like, uh, for example, making clothes by hand, or building house in, like, old way. Not many people know this now. This is a problem, I think. Because traditional skill is important for, um, our culture.\n\nAnd government role? Uh, I think government can, uh, help. Maybe they give money to people who do this, um, old work. Or, they can, uh, teach in school. For young people to learn. So, uh, they not forget. Yes. Government must do something for this. Is good for country, I think.",
    "mid": "Yes, I think it is true that many traditional skills are being lost. For example, like the old way to make clothes, or special food. Because society is getting more modern, with new technology, people don't need these skills much.\n\nI think it is a concern, but not very serious for everyone. For me, it's a little bit serious. We lose our culture and history if young people don't learn these old things. So, it's important for our identity.\n\nFor government, they should help. Maybe give money for schools to teach crafts or special cooking. Also, government can make, um, festivals to show old skills. This help people remember. So yes, government has a role.",
    "high": "I think the concern about losing traditional skills is undeniably serious. As society modernizes, we risk losing not only practical abilities but also a significant part of our cultural heritage and identity. Skills like artisanal crafts or traditional farming techniques hold intrinsic value, connecting us to our past and offering unique knowledge that could be vital for future sustainability. Once lost, they are incredibly difficult, if not impossible, to revive, making this a critical cultural and practical issue.\n\nGovernments absolutely have a proactive role to play. Their function shouldn't be to halt progress, but rather to safeguard these valuable traditions. This could involve offering funding and subsidies for vocational training in traditional crafts, integrating relevant practical skills into school curricula, or supporting community-led apprenticeships. Additionally, promoting cultural tourism around these skills could make them economically viable. It's essentially about creating an environment where these skills can naturally thrive and be passed on, rather than forcibly preserving them."
  },
  "5796818f-0ecb-4125-b959-2ce9737b0585": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Um, I think still we have national cultures, yes. Is meaningful to talk about them. Because every country have their own special things, you know? Like, um, food. Thai food is very different from Italian food, yes? And also music. Some countries have very traditional music, like folk music, very different than pop music from America. And festivals, yes. In my country, we have many special festivals, other countries don't have this. So, even if now we see same clothes, same phone everywhere, but inside, the culture is still there. People like their own ways to do things. So, I think is important to speak about this. Yes, we still have different cultures, is not all same.",
    "mid": "Well, I think it's a good question. Um, for me, yes, I think it is still meaningful to speak about distinct national cultures. Even though the world is becoming more globalised, people still have strong connections to their own country and traditions.\n\nFor example, we see many international brands everywhere, like McDonald's or Starbucks. But still, each country has very unique food culture. In Italy, they eat pasta very often, and in my country, we have our own special dishes that are very different from, uh, other places. And also, traditions for holidays, like Christmas or New Year, they are celebrated in different ways in different countries. This shows that people still keep their own national identity. So, yes, I believe it's meaningful to talk about them.",
    "high": "That's a really insightful question. While globalisation undeniably blurs many cultural lines – we see similar fashion, music, and fast-food everywhere – suggesting a convergence, I strongly believe it's still incredibly meaningful to speak of distinct national cultures.\n\nDespite superficial merging, deeper elements persist. Consider a country's legal system, core social values, or historical narratives embedded in language and education. For instance, the collectivist ethos prevalent in East Asian societies contrasts sharply with Western individualism. Similarly, unique nuances of humour or social etiquette specific to, say, British versus American cultures are not easily erased. These deeply ingrained aspects continue to shape identity and interaction, affirming that distinct national cultures absolutely persist."
  },
  "3542db1b-84b4-476f-b3ae-130269536fb6": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay. So, I must to read this, uh, sentence. Hmm. It say: 'I *wood* like to... *stoo-dy*... *Eeng-lish*... at a... uni-VER-si-ty... be-coss... it is... an *in-TER-nash-ional*... lan-gu-age.' Uh... is that good? I try again. 'I *wooould*... like to... study... *Eenglish*... at a... *uni-VER-sity*... be-cause... it is... an... *in-ter-NA-tion-al*... lan-gu-age.' Is very difficult for me to... uh... put the stress right. I don't know always where is the main... uh, part. But I think, maybe, 'university' is a bit *VER* and 'international' is a bit *NA*? Is hard. My pronun-ciation is not... good, I think. But I try my best for this.",
    "mid": "Um, okay. So I just read 'I would like to study English at a university because it is an international language.' I think this sentence is very popular idea. Many people think like this. For me also, I want to improve my English, definitely. About the word stress, um, when I said 'international', I think it is 'interNAAtional', yes? And 'university', I try for 'uniVERsity'. It is difficult sometimes to make it exactly right, but I try to pay attention. English is very important, because everywhere you go, like, for business or for travel, many people speak English. So, to study it at a university, it gives you good opportunity to learn more deeply, and to communicate better. I think this is a very strong reason for many people to do that.",
    "high": "Okay, I've just read the sentence aloud, focusing on the stress. In the phrase, 'I would like to study English at a university because it is an international language,' I naturally put stress on the main content words. For example, 'like' expresses my preference, then 'study,' 'English,' and 'university' are key for understanding the action and place. Towards the end, 'international' and 'language' receive significant emphasis because they provide the core reason. Words like 'would,' 'at,' 'a,' and 'it is' are typically unstressed, flowing more quickly. This placement of stress is crucial for conveying the meaning clearly and naturally, highlighting the important information for the listener and ensuring good intelligibility."
  },
  "eefa98d4-436d-4ab4-9ad8-d7a21406fc67": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay. So, um, I use the words from the pairs now. For the first one, 'ship' or 'sheep', I want to use 'ship'. My sentence is, 'The big ship go on the water, many peoples is on it.' Then, for 'bit' and 'beat', I choose 'bit'. I say, 'Can I have a little bit of that cake, please? I like cake very much.' Next, 'fit' and 'feet'. I want to use 'fit'. 'This dress not fit me, it's too small for my body.' And the last one, 'live' or 'leave'. I choose 'live'. 'I live in a small town near the mountains with my parents, is good.'",
    "mid": "Okay, I will choose one word from each pair now.\n\nFirst, for 'ship' and 'sheep', I choose 'ship'. My friend's father works on a big *ship*. He travels many countries for his job.\n\nThen, for 'bit' and 'beat', I choose 'bit'. Can I have a *bit* of your food? I'm very hungry because I didn't eat lunch.\n\nNext, for 'fit' and 'feet', I pick 'fit'. I go to the gym to keep myself *fit*. It's important for my good health, I think.\n\nAnd last, for 'live' and 'leave', I will use 'live'. I *live* in apartment complex, uh, near the university. It is convenient for my studies every day.",
    "high": "Well, I've certainly tried to distinguish the sounds of those pairs. So, taking one from each, for 'ship' and 'sheep', I'll use 'ship'. I actually saw a very large cargo ship sailing down the river just yesterday. Moving on to 'bit' and 'beat', I've chosen 'beat'. I often find myself tapping my feet to the beat of a catchy song. Then for 'fit' and 'feet', I think 'feet' is a good choice for a sentence. My feet were incredibly sore after walking around the entire exhibition hall today. And finally, from 'live' and 'leave', I'll use 'live'. I currently live in a flat on the third floor, which is quite nice because it has a good view. I hope these examples are clear."
  },
  "4ac0cdc5-efc5-4a60-965d-3989ed1c470b": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I... I try to read the passage. For the second part, what is difficult for me to pronounce... uhm, I think many sounds in English is very difficult. For example, the 'th' sound, like in 'think' or 'this'. My tongue is not... not good to make this sound. Sometimes I say 'sink' instead of 'think'. It is a problem for me. Also, the 'r' sound. It is very different from my language. I... I try to say it, but it come out not clear. And also many words with many consonants together, like 'world' or 'strength'. It is very hard for me to say it good. And also, the vowels. Many different vowel sounds, like 'i' and 'ee'. Sometimes they sound same for me. Yes. Many challenges for me in speaking this language.",
    "mid": "Hmm, for me, what I find difficult to pronounce in English… I think is mostly the sounds that don't exist in my own language. Like, for example, the 'th' sound. It is really hard for my tongue to make this sound, like in 'three' or 'clothes'. Sometimes I say 'tree' or 'cloze' instead. Also, I have a problem with some vowel sounds, like 'sheep' and 'ship'. They sound very similar to me, and it can be confuse to choose the correct one. And also word stress, like when I say a word with two syllables. For example, 'present' as a noun or verb, sometimes I put the stress on the wrong part, and this can make people not understand me easily. Yes, these are my main difficulties.",
    "high": "Okay, explaining what I find difficult to pronounce... I'd say there are a few areas that consistently challenge me. Firstly, distinguishing between certain vowel sounds, particularly the short 'i' in 'sit' and the long 'ee' in 'seat'. Sometimes I struggle to produce them distinctly, which can occasionally lead to misunderstandings, especially with similar-sounding words.\n\nSecondly, I often find consonant clusters, especially at the end of words, quite tricky. For instance, words like 'strengths' or 'clothes' involve multiple consonants coming together, and my tongue sometimes gets tangled trying to articulate them clearly and smoothly.\n\nAnd finally, getting the correct word stress and intonation in longer sentences is a constant effort. While I generally understand the rules, applying them naturally and consistently in spontaneous speech requires a lot of practice to sound truly native-like."
  },
  "240c97cb-4220-4495-a0d9-3c50260015b6": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I will try to read these words. PRO-duce. And then pro-DUCE. Next is RE-cord. And re-CORD. I hope I say it right. Now, I make sentences.\n\nMy sentence for PRO-duce, the noun, is: The farmer sell many good produce, uh, like vegetable, at the market.\n\nFor pro-DUCE, the verb: This big factory produce many car and phone every day. They work very fast.\n\nMy sentence for RE-cord, the noun: I like to listen old music record. My father have many old one.\n\nAnd for re-CORD, the verb: Can you please record this movie for me? I want to watch it later tonight. Thank you.",
    "mid": "Okay, I will give the sentences for these words.\n\nFirst, PRO-duce, the noun. My local shop sell very good fresh PRO-duce, like apples and carrots. All this PRO-duce is from farms near here.\n\nThen, pro-DUCE, the verb. Our company want to pro-DUCE more of their products next year. They have to pro-DUCE good quality.\n\nNext, RE-cord, the noun. My brother, he love to collect old RE-cords. He has many valuable RE-cords in his room.\n\nAnd for re-CORD, the verb. Can you please re-CORD the news for me? I miss it yesterday, so I will re-CORD this important lecture.",
    "high": "Okay, so I will try to use them now. First, for the noun form, PRO-duce, I would say, 'The farmers' market always has the freshest local produce available, particularly during the summer season.' You know, it's really important to support local agriculture. And then, for the verb form, pro-DUCE, I might say, 'Many large corporations nowadays produce a wide variety of electronic gadgets, exporting them globally to meet consumer demand across different continents.' I think that clearly shows the difference in meaning and, of course, the stress."
  },
  "aaac1a9b-e531-4c70-a61b-3378c936b184": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I read the sentence. I think I use, um, like, linking sounds. For example, 'gone to', I connect the 'n' with the 'o' sound. And 'meeting if', I link the 'g' and the 'i'. Also 'known about', I put 'n' and 'a' sound together. This make the speech faster.\n\nFor weak forms, I use 'to' like 'tuh', not 'too'. 'But' also become very short, like 'buh'. Especially 'I'd've', this is very quick, like, 'Id've', not 'I would have'. I think is for speak more quick and natural. I also try to make sounds smooth, like 'until yesterday'. So, linking and make short words. Yes.",
    "mid": "Okay, I just read the sentence. I tried to use connected speech features when I say it. For linking, I linked words like 'meeting if', so the 'g' and 'i' sound connect together. And also 'about it', the 't' from 'about' goes with 'it'. It makes the speech flow.\n\nFor weak forms, I definitely make 'I'd've' sound like 'eye-duv', which is a much shorter way to say 'I would have'. And also 'to' became 'tuh', very short.\n\nFor assimilation, I think in 'but nobody', the 't' sound from 'but' kind of change and blend with 'nobody'. It's not a strong 't' sound, it sound like a 'n' more. This helps to speak more quickly and naturally, I think.",
    "high": "Okay, I would say that sentence quite quickly and smoothly. For instance, \"I'd've\" would be heavily reduced, sounding almost like \"I-dəv\" or \"eye-duh-vuh\", clearly showing the weak forms of 'would have'. The words \"to the\" would also be weak, becoming \"tuh-thuh\", and connecting with \"gone\", so it flows as \"gone-tuh-thuh meeting\".\n\nI'd apply linking extensively. For example, \"meeting if\" would link the final 'g' sound of 'meeting' to the 'i' of 'if'. Similarly, \"known about\" connects the 'n' to the 'a' sound. And \"about it\" would link the 't' sound to the 'i'. Assimilation might happen with \"until yesterday\", where the 'l' could slightly change to a 'y' sound, or even the 'd' in \"I'd known\" could assimilate with 'known'. Overall, the aim is to blend words together for natural rhythm and speed."
  },
  "ffbcaa73-80bb-4139-b101-30ceecfaa416": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Uh, for me, I think, uh, both is important, but, uh, maybe pronunciation, uh, is, uh, more important. Yes. Because, uh, if you, uh, speak and, uh, people cannot understand you, then, uh, is no communication. So, uh, you must be clear with your words. For example, uh, if I say 'sheep' but, uh, I say 'ship', then is, uh, different thing. So, understand is hard. Uh, fluency also good, yes. If you talk, uh, no stop much, it's, uh, good. But, uh, if you talk very fast, and, uh, people don't understand, uh, what you say, then, uh, it's not good communication. So, uh, I think, first, uh, must be clear speaking, then, uh, fluency. That's what I think.",
    "mid": "I think both are very important, but I believe fluency is a little bit more... crucial for good communication. If you speak very fluently, even if your pronunciation has small errors, people can still understand your general meaning. You can keep talking and explain your idea better. For example, if your speech is smooth, the listener can follow your main points.\n\nHowever, pronunciation is also very important. If your pronunciation is really bad, like, people cannot understand any words, it causes big misunderstanding. The listener cannot know which word you say. So, you must be understood. But with good fluency, you can often explain around a pronunciation mistake, making the conversation flow better. So, both are important, but fluency sometimes helps more.",
    "high": "That's an interesting question. I'd say both are incredibly important, but perhaps in different ways. Fluency, for me, is paramount for effective spoken communication because it allows you to convey your ideas naturally and keep the conversation flowing. If you're constantly hesitating or stopping to find words, it can be really challenging for the listener to follow your train of thought, even if your individual words are perfectly pronounced.\n\nHowever, pronunciation accuracy also plays a crucial role. If your pronunciation is too far off, you risk serious misunderstandings, which can completely break down communication, even if you're speaking fluently. For example, mispronouncing key words could change the meaning entirely. So, while I think fluency might initially engage the listener more, accurate pronunciation ensures that what you're saying is actually understood. It's truly a balance."
  },
  "cb7fa3ca-9359-4b11-83e9-346cd743d81f": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "My daily routine is very simple. Every day, I wake up at 7 o'clock. Then I eat breakfast, usually bread and coffee fast. After that, I go to my job. I work in a shop from 9 AM to 5 PM. It is very busy. For lunch, I eat sandwich quickly. When I finish work, I go home. I cook dinner for myself, often rice and chicken. In the evening, I watch TV for some time or read book. Then I go to bed around 10 o'clock. I need to sleep many hours. This is my normal life every week.\n\nLast month, I have my last holiday. My routine was very different then. **Before**, I always wake up early for work. But during holiday, I wake up very late, like 10 AM. I do not eat breakfast at home. Instead, I eat on restaurant with my family, it was delicious. My job was not there, so I do not work at all. **After** breakfast, we went to beach or to museum. We spent many hours outside and walked a lot. For dinner, we ate in different places every night, trying new food. I did not watch TV much because we are outside. I sleep later than normal too. It was very relaxing for me. My holiday routine was more fun than my normal routine.",
    "mid": "My daily routine is quite busy during the week. I usually wake up at half past six in the morning. **First**, I drink a coffee and eat a light breakfast with bread. **After that**, I leave my home for work. I work in an office from nine in the morning until five in the evening. **Then**, when I come back home, I often cook dinner and do some house chores, like wash the dishes. **Before** I go to bed, I like to read a book or watch a little television for relaxing time.\n\nMy last holiday was very different from this routine. I went to the beach for a week. I did not wake up so early. I woke up around nine or ten o'clock every day, which was very nice. I did not have to go to work, so I spent my mornings on the beach. I swam in the sea and walked along the sand with my friend. **While** I was on holiday, I ate all my meals in restaurants, enjoying local food. In the afternoon, I usually explored the small town or just relaxed by the pool, sometimes reading. I slept much more and felt very happy because I had no responsibilities. It was a great change from my daily life.",
    "high": "My daily routine is usually quite structured, primarily revolving around my job. I typically wake up at 7:00 AM, have a quick breakfast, and then commute to the office, arriving by 8:30 AM. My workday involves attending meetings, working on projects, and responding to emails. After work, around 6:00 PM, I often go to the gym or meet friends for dinner. I usually prepare for the next day before going to bed by 11:00 PM.\n\nHowever, my last holiday brought a delightful change to this predictable schedule. Before the holiday, my mornings were rushed, but during my trip to the coast, I enjoyed waking up much later, often after 9:00 AM. While on holiday, instead of commuting to an office, my mornings involved leisurely walks along the beach or exploring local markets. My afternoons were spent swimming or reading a book, a stark contrast to my usual work tasks. Evenings were also different; we dined out at various restaurants, enjoying local cuisine until late, without worrying about an early start the next day.\n\nThis shift from a disciplined work schedule to a relaxed, spontaneous holiday routine was incredibly refreshing, allowing me to fully unwind and recharge."
  },
  "eada7970-0c6d-4faa-ac94-773d905ae8ce": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "My hometown name is a small city. In the past, it was very quiet place. There were many trees and green parks everywhere. The houses were mostly old and big, with gardens. Few cars on the street, so children played outside safe. Everyone knew each other, like a big family. My hometown was peaceful, and life was slow.\n\nNow, my hometown is much bigger and more busy. There are new buildings, tall apartments, and more shops. The roads have many cars and often traffic jams. Some green areas disappeared for construct new buildings. People are still friendly, but not everyone know each other like before. It has a small shopping mall and a few fast food restaurants now. Life is faster and more noisy.\n\nI think in the future, my hometown will become even bigger. There will be more high-rise buildings and maybe a very large shopping center. I hope it will keep some green places, but I worry it will become too crowded and polluted. Maybe it will have a train station one day. I think it will lose its quiet charm and become like other big cities.",
    "mid": "My hometown, it was a very small village. In the past, there was many green fields and big trees around. Most people was farmers, and our life was very quiet. We had only a one small shop and a school. Everyone know their neighbours, and children played in the street safely. There was not much traffic, just few bicycles and old cars.\n\nNow, my hometown is much bigger. It is not a village anymore, it is a town. There are many new building, like small factories and apartment blocks. The green fields gone, replaced by houses. More cars make traffic busy, and sometimes the air is not fresh. We have more shops, a big supermarket, and new hospital. People work in the factories or in the new shops now. The community feeling is less strong than before it was.\n\nIn the future, I think my hometown will become even bigger, like a small city. There will be more tall buildings and more traffic. Maybe a university will come here, providing more jobs for young people. But I worry that it will lose its quiet charm and become too noisy and crowded place. It will be a very different place, perhaps with more opportunities but with less natural beauty.",
    "high": "My hometown, Greenfield, was once a small, quiet agricultural town in the heart of the countryside. In the 1990s, it comprised mostly farms and a modest town centre with only a few local shops. Life moved at a slower, more relaxed pace; neighbours knew each other well, and children often played freely in the surrounding fields. The community was close-knit, and transport links were limited, which made it feel quite isolated.\n\nToday, Greenfield has transformed significantly. It is now a bustling commuter town with a much larger population, accommodating people working in the nearby city. Many new housing estates have been built, and the old town centre has been redeveloped, boasting several supermarkets, cafes, and even a shopping mall. While the sense of community has become somewhat diluted due to the influx of new residents, public services like schools and healthcare facilities have vastly improved. Traffic congestion, however, is now a noticeable problem during peak hours.\n\nLooking ahead, I believe Greenfield will continue its rapid expansion. Planners have proposed further residential and commercial developments, and I anticipate that the town’s infrastructure, particularly roads and public transport, will need considerable upgrades to cope. It is likely that more green spaces will be sacrificed for development, which is a concern. Nevertheless, I hope the local council will implement sustainable initiatives to preserve some of its original charm while embracing necessary modernisation."
  },
  "dd95f5e4-4943-4e1e-b0f6-06d11c337ec5": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Some students think it is best to study alone. But other students believe that learning with a group is more effective. This essay will discuss these two different opinions on how students learn.\n\nFor some students, studying by themselves is the most good way. They can concentrate very well because there is no noise or distraction. When a student is alone, he can choose to study at his own speed, so he don't feel pressure from other people. He can also repeat difficult parts many times until he understand. For example, if he is reading a hard book, he can read it slow. It is good if you want to focus deep.\n\nHowever, some other students prefer to study in groups. This is because they can share ideas with friends. If a student does not understand something, another student can explain it. They can discuss new topics and get different views from each other, whereas sometimes one person's idea is not enough. Group study also makes learning more fun for some people, although it can be noisy sometimes. They can help each other for homework and projects too.\n\nIn conclusion, both ways of studying have their good points. Studying alone is good for deep focus, and group study helps with discussion and sharing. The best way is depend on what each student feel comfortable with.",
    "mid": "Students have different ways to learn, and some prefer to study alone while others like to work in groups. This choice often depends on individual learning styles and the specific subject.\n\nSome students find that studying by themselves is more effective because they can concentrate without distractions. When a student is alone, he can set his own pace and focus only on what he need to learn. This method allows for deeper thought and understanding of complex ideas without the need to compromise with others or wait for group members. Although some people might find it boring, this method helps to go deep into subjects and understand difficult concepts better.\n\nHowever, other students believe that learning in groups is very useful. They can discuss topics and share their thoughts, which can help to understand different points of view. If a student struggles with a problem, the other group members often explain it to him, so it is easier to solve. Whereas some individuals may feel shy to speak in a group, many find that explaining concepts to others can strengthen their own understanding and highlight areas they still need to learn.\n\nIn conclusion, both studying alone and studying in groups have their own advantages. The best method often depends on the student's personality and the subject they are learning. What works for one person might not work for another.",
    "high": "Many students hold differing opinions regarding the most effective study methods. While some learners strongly advocate for solitary study, others find collaborative group work more conducive to their academic success.\n\nThose who prefer studying alone often argue that it allows for unparalleled concentration and a personalised pace. They can delve deeply into complex subjects without interruption, which often leads to a more profound understanding of the material. Furthermore, individual study enables students to tailor their learning strategy to their specific needs, revisiting challenging topics as many times as necessary without feeling rushed or obligated to others. This method is particularly effective for analytical tasks or memorisation, because it minimises external distractions.\n\nConversely, proponents of group study highlight the benefits of diverse perspectives and shared knowledge. Learning in groups can foster critical discussion and problem-solving, as students explain concepts to each other, which in itself reinforces understanding. Although some individuals find group sessions distracting, others thrive in this interactive environment, benefiting from immediate feedback and different approaches to a problem. If students actively participate and manage their time effectively, they can clarify doubts and strengthen their knowledge collaboratively.\n\nUltimately, the choice between solitary and group study largely depends on an individual's learning style and the nature of the academic task. Both approaches offer distinct advantages, whereas their effectiveness can vary significantly from person to person."
  },
  "efcaaa30-7a42-4db1-b22d-43025ce2c255": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "If I could change one rule at my school, it would be the rule about mobile phones. Currently, students are not allowed to use their phones in class or even during break times. This rule, which is very strict, make many students unhappy and sometimes bored, because they cannot communicate with family or check news.\n\nI think this is not a good rule because phones can be very useful for study. If students are only using phones for games, then it is a problem that needs to stop. But if they can use it for learning, like finding information or translating difficult words, it is very helpful for everyone. For example, if a teacher asks a hard question in class, and we don't know the answer, we cannot search it fast. If we had our phones, we could find the information quickly, which would help us to understand the lesson better in that moment.\n\nAlso, if the school had allowed phones earlier, maybe students would learned how to use them responsible for education. Many new apps and websites exist for learning, that students could use to improve their skills. Students who want to do extra research for projects must wait until they are home, which is not always convenient. If the school changes this rule, I believe students will be more interested in lessons and get better marks for their effort. It is important that a school trusts its students. This change will make school a more modern and effective place for everyone who want to learn new things.",
    "mid": "If I could change one rule at my workplace, it would be the fixed working hours. Currently, everyone must work from 9 AM to 5 PM, which is very strict for many people. This is a rule that cause a lot of stress for employees.\n\nIf employees were allow to choose their start and end times, they would feel much more happier. For example, some people needs to take their children to school or have doctor appointments. If they have a flexible schedule, they can manage their personal life better. Many colleagues, who has family responsibilities, find the current system difficult.\n\nIf the company introduce this flexibility, staff will be more productive. People works better when they are not worried about personal commitments. If the management had considered this idea earlier, they would have probably seen less resignations and more motivated staff. This change would not only benefit the workers but also the company, leading to a more positive work environment which improve overall performance.",
    "high": "If I were given the opportunity to alter one regulation at my current workplace, I would undoubtedly revise the stringent policy on mandatory office attendance. This rule, which currently dictates that all employees must be physically present five days a week, often hinders both productivity and overall job satisfaction.\n\nIf this rule were changed, and we adopted a more hybrid approach, employees would experience significantly improved work-life balance. For instance, if people could work remotely a few days a week, they would save considerable commuting time and costs, which are major stressors. This flexibility, which is highly valued by modern professionals, would likely lead to higher morale and fewer instances of burnout. Furthermore, if individuals had more autonomy over their working location, their overall job satisfaction would certainly increase.\n\nThe organization itself would also benefit greatly. If we had implemented this flexibility earlier, I believe the company would have attracted and retained more top talent who actively seek such modern arrangements. Furthermore, if fewer people commuted daily, the company would likely see reduced utility costs, and it would contribute positively to environmental sustainability, a factor which many companies now prioritize. If employees feel trusted and empowered by this change, their loyalty and commitment to the company will undoubtedly grow.\n\nIn conclusion, altering the mandatory office attendance policy would bring about numerous advantages. If this change were adopted, it would foster a more productive, satisfied, and environmentally conscious workforce, which ultimately benefits everyone involved."
  },
  "bcb3d1ac-a08a-40eb-97ef-251d3f26a1f7": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Many people talk about if government should like public transport more than private cars. I think this is a good idea for government to do.\n\nFirst, public transport helps the city because it makes less cars on the road. When there are less cars, the traffic is not so bad for everyone. Also, public transport is better for the air. The pollution is made by many cars driving alone, but a bus can carry many peoples at one time, so it make less bad air overall. So, for the environment, public transport is very good. Money can be saved by many peoples too when they not buy expensive car and pay for fuel everyday. This saving can help families much.\n\nBut private cars are also good for some people. They give freedom and people can go to work fast. But if government help public transport, maybe more people will use it. New buses should be bought and more train lines. It is believed that more people will use buses if they are good. This investment in public transport is good for all.\n\nSo, I very much agree that government must make public transport a priority. It is better for environment and traffic and save peoples money.",
    "mid": "Governments should definitely make public transport a priority more than private car ownership. I strongly agree with this statement because it brings many important benefits for a country and its people.\n\nFirstly, a main advantage is the positive impact on environment. If many people travel by buses or trains, less individual cars are used. This leads to a significant reduction of carbon emissions, which is very important for fighting global warming. Less air pollution is also good for everyone's health in cities. Huge amounts of money should be invested into developing this green transportation.\n\nSecondly, public transport helps to reduce traffic congestion. In many big cities, roads are often full, causing long delays for commuters. By providing efficient public transport services, the number of cars on roads can be reduced. This makes travel faster and more reliable for everyone. Also, for many people, the cost of using public transport is much cheaper than owning a private car, including fuel and parking fees.\n\nHowever, it is true that private cars offer flexibility, especially for people living in rural areas where public transport is not always available. But for urban centers, public transport can meet the needs of most citizens effectively.\n\nIn conclusion, it is clear that governments ought to prioritise public transport. It is better for the environment, helps solve traffic problems, and is more economical for citizens. This investment will make a better future.",
    "high": "It is widely contended that governmental focus should shift towards enhancing public transportation networks rather than facilitating individual car ownership. While the convenience offered by private vehicles is undeniable, I largely agree that a primary emphasis on public transport systems brings significant societal and environmental advantages.\n\nA strong case for this prioritisation can be made regarding environmental sustainability. A substantial reduction in carbon emissions and urban air pollution is achieved when a greater proportion of the population utilises buses, trains, and trams. Furthermore, traffic congestion, a perpetual issue in modern cities, would be considerably alleviated, leading to economic benefits through improved productivity and reduced fuel consumption. Such developments also promote greater social equity, as accessibility is extended to those without personal vehicles.\n\nHowever, it is also important to acknowledge that the complete abolition of private car ownership is neither practical nor desirable in all circumstances. In rural areas, where public transport infrastructure may be insufficient, private vehicles remain a necessity for many. Moreover, certain professions require the flexibility and cargo capacity that only a private car can provide.\n\nIn conclusion, although the role of private cars cannot be entirely dismissed, particularly in specific geographical contexts, the advantages of a robust public transport system – encompassing environmental protection, economic efficiency, and social inclusion – far outweigh the benefits of unchecked individual car use. Therefore, governmental efforts should decisively lean towards the enhancement of public transport."
  },
  "9e0bec92-1cc7-455e-bb1a-96e8f5c86bca": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Yes, the world is very different if the internet was not invented. It is true.\n\nFirst, communication for people would be very difficult. Now, we can send email very fast or call with video to family far away. If no internet, people must write letter, and this is very slow. Or maybe use a normal phone call, but it cost more money. We cannot talk to friend so easy every day.\n\nAlso, for finding information, it would be much harder. There is no Google search for students to do homework fast. They must go to library for book, or ask teachers. News from other country would be slow to get. We cannot learn new things quickly from other places.\n\nMany jobs today need the internet. For example, online shops or meetings with people in other cities. If no internet, these kind of work cannot exist. Many people would lose job, or have to travel very far for work. Daily life would be much more slow. We cannot see social media or share pictures with friends easily.\n\nMaybe people would talk face-to-face more often, or go outside more. But many good things, like fast learning and easy communication, would be gone. So, the world is very, very different place if internet not made. It make our life easy but also we depend on it too much now.",
    "mid": "The internet has dramatically changed our world, and it is clear that if it had never been invented, the world would be a very different place today. This technology is so important for many aspects of our daily lives.\n\nFirstly, communication would be much slower. If the internet was never invented, people would still need to use traditional methods like letters or making long-distance phone calls to talk with family and friends who live far away. Sending emails, which is a very fast way, would not exist. So, our connection with others, especially those in other countries, would be less immediate and personal. We also would not have social media platforms, making it difficult to keep up with news and daily activities of friends.\n\nFurthermore, how we get information and learn would be very different. Had the internet not appeared, students and researchers would only rely on books in libraries for their studies. Researching for school projects would take a lot of time and be very difficult. Also, many jobs now depend heavily on the internet. Without it, businesses would find it hard to communicate globally and to share important data fast. People could not work from their homes easily too. Therefore, the global economy would be slower and not so connected like it is now.\n\nIn conclusion, the internet has brought many big changes. If it had never been invented, our modern lives would be more complicated and less efficient, really showing how much we rely on this technology now.",
    "high": "Had the internet never been invented, the world would undoubtedly be a profoundly different place. It is clear that this ubiquitous technology has fundamentally reshaped nearly every facet of human existence, from global communication to economic structures.\n\nIf the internet had never been conceived, our methods of information dispersal and interpersonal communication would remain significantly more antiquated. Individuals, rather than instantly connecting across continents, would still rely on slower, more localised means such as traditional mail or landlines, meaning many cross-cultural exchanges now commonplace would not have occurred. Furthermore, had it not existed, access to vast repositories of knowledge, which underpins modern education and scientific research, would be severely limited, making collective problem-solving on a global scale far more challenging than it is today.\n\nEconomically, had global digital networks not emerged, the entire landscape of commerce would be unrecognisable. E-commerce would be non-existent, and the concept of remote working, which has proven so vital recently, could never have developed. Consequently, urbanisation patterns might differ, and the 'global village' we currently inhabit, where economies and cultures are tightly interwoven, would not have fully materialised. Never would we have witnessed such an acceleration in globalisation.\n\nIn conclusion, it is evident that the internet's absence would leave an unparalleled void in modern life. The interconnected, fast-paced world we experience, where information is instantaneously accessible and global collaboration is routine, simply would not exist."
  },
  "5a01a829-5499-432a-9246-b06daf066c0a": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "My favourite meal is chicken with rice. This meal is so special for me, my mother always cook it for my birthday. She is very good cook.\n\nFor this meal, she use big chicken parts. First, she marinate the chicken in many spices, like red chilli powder and salt. Then, she fry it in hot oil until it turn golden brown. The skin of chicken is crispy and crunchy, but inside, the meat is so tender and juicy. It has a little spicy taste and also salty. The aroma while cooking is very strong and delicious.\n\nWe eat the fried chicken with plain white rice. The rice is steamed, so it is soft and fluffy. Sometimes, my mother adds some small pieces of carrot in the rice, they become sweet and soft when cooked. I like to mix the chicken with rice, the flavour is very good together. It fills my stomach. This food is very comforting for me. I enjoy it always.",
    "mid": "My most favourite meal is a homemade lasagna. It is a very comforting dish that my mother often cook for special occasions, and I also try to make it sometimes.\n\nThe main ingredients of lasagna are thin pasta sheets layered with a rich meat sauce and a creamy white sauce, then lots of cheese. For the meat sauce, we use minced beef, which is first browned with chopped onions and garlic. Then, crushed tomatoes, passata, and fresh herbs like basil and oregano are added, simmered for a long time until it become a thick, savoury ragu. The white sauce, or béchamel, is made with butter, flour, and milk, cooked until it is smooth and very creamy.\n\nWhen it bakes in the oven, the smell is wonderful. The taste is a complex mix of flavours. The tomato sauce is tangy and a little sweet, blending perfectly with the salty and umami taste of the beef. The béchamel sauce adds a smooth, milky richness, making each bite feel indulgent. The texture is also amazing: the pasta sheets are tender, the meat filling is soft, and the melted mozzarella and parmesan cheese on top form a gooey, golden crust that is slightly crispy. It is a deeply satisfying meal that always makes me happy.",
    "high": "My ultimate comfort food and undisputed favourite meal is a meticulously prepared homemade lasagna. This classic Italian dish, with its numerous layers and rich flavours, never fails to satisfy my palate and offers a profound sense of comfort.\n\nThe foundation of a perfect lasagna begins with a slow-simmered bolognese sauce, featuring finely minced beef, sautéed onions, garlic, and carrots, all stewed in a rich passata made from ripe San Marzano tomatoes. This base offers a deep umami taste, beautifully complemented by the aromatic notes of dried oregano and fresh basil. Layered alternately are sheets of pasta, cooked *al dente*, and a creamy, velvety béchamel sauce, infused with a subtle hint of nutmeg. The texture journey is delightful: the tender beef and soft vegetables contrast beautifully with the firm pasta.\n\nFurthermore, generous amounts of freshly grated Parmesan and mild, stretchy mozzarella cheese are dispersed throughout, creating pockets of salty, tangy richness that melt luxuriously when baked. The entire creation culminates in a gratinéed top, where the cheese turns golden and slightly crisp, adding a delightful crunch to each forkful. Paired with a simple crisp green salad, this meal is a symphony of savoury tastes and varied textures, making it an exquisite culinary experience."
  },
  "17de0bdf-7f37-4218-9f6f-ad138314f0eb": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "I would like to be a chef in future. This is a very interesting job for me. I like to cook since I am a child. Making food for people make me happy. A chef prepare different dishes in the kitchen. I enjoy to see people happy when they eat my food.\n\nA chef need many skills. He must be creative and fast. Also, a chef need to be clean and organized. I think I am good at cooking already. I can cut many vegetables quickly. I also like to learn new recipe. This job is hard work because long hours. But I don't mind to work hard for something I love. I want to create delicious meals for customers.\n\nTo become a good chef, I must study. I will go to a cooking school after my high school. There, I will learn professional techniques and how to manage a kitchen. I want to work in a big restaurant someday. I believe with much practice, I will be a successful cook. This occupation is my dream for my future life. It is a rewarding career path.",
    "mid": "My future aspiration is to pursue a career in nursing. I believe this occupation is very important and meaningful for society. A nurse's primary duty is to care for patients who are unwell, offering them support and medical assistance.\n\nThe daily tasks of a nurse involve many responsibilities. They often administer medication, monitor vital signs, and communicate with doctors about patient conditions. To perform these duties effectively, several important abilities are required. A successful practitioner in this field needs strong communication skills to talk with patients and their families, and also empathy to understand their suffering. Furthermore, critical thinking is essential for making quick decisions in emergency situations.\n\nI am drawn to this profession because I have a strong desire to assist others and contribute positively to people’s health. While it can be a challenging job, I think the satisfaction from helping others to recover make it a truly rewarding vocation. I am planning to study diligently to achieve the necessary qualifications for this vital work.",
    "high": "My future career aspiration is to become a management consultant. This profession deeply appeals to me due to its dynamic nature and intellectual challenges.\n\nThe core responsibility of a management consultant involves assisting diverse organizations in resolving critical issues and enhancing their performance. This undertaking demands a robust analytical capability to diagnose problems, alongside exceptional problem-solving skills to devise effective solutions. Furthermore, adept communication and interpersonal proficiencies are paramount for engaging with clients, understanding their needs, and effectively conveying complex recommendations. This vocational path necessitates constant learning and adaptation.\n\nWhat draws me most to this role is the opportunity to contribute meaningfully to various enterprises across different sectors. I am particularly keen on leveraging my critical thinking aptitude to tackle intricate business dilemmas. The prospect of working with an array of specialists and fostering innovative strategies is incredibly stimulating. Moreover, this career promises continuous professional development, as each new assignment presents unique obstacles and learning experiences, which I find highly gratifying.\n\nUltimately, pursuing a career as a management consultant aligns perfectly with my desire for a challenging, impactful, and perpetually evolving professional journey."
  },
  "833697ce-6be0-477b-8b91-181d83255136": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Many people today buying things they not need. This is a big problem in our society now. There are some cause and effect for this.\n\nOne cause is the advertise. Companies make many advertisement on TV and internet. They show very good things and people want them. Also, social media is a cause. People see friends buy new clothes or phone, then they want to buy too. They feel they need to have same things. Sometimes people feel sad or bored, so they go shopping. This make them happy for a short time. This is like an impulse purchase.\n\nThe effect is not good. First, people spend much money. They do not have money for real need like food or rent. Some people go to debt because they buy many things. Also, it is bad for environment. When people buy many things they don't use, they throw away later. This makes much rubbish. Homes become full of things, no space. This is a problem for many people.\n\nIn conclusion, many advertise and social media makes people buy things they do not need. This cause money problems and bad for earth.",
    "mid": "Today, many people around the world purchase products that they do not truly need. This trend has several causes and significant effects on both individuals and the environment.\n\nOne main cause of this consumer behaviour is advertising. Companies use clever marketing strategies, especially on social media, to make people want new things. Also, social pressure plays a role; people feel they need to buy certain items to fit in or keep up with friends who show off their latest purchases. The availability of easy credit and credit cards also encourages people to spend more money than they have, leading to many impulse purchases. Many people have more disposable income than before, so they spend it on luxury items instead of necessities.\n\nThe effects of buying unnecessary items are widespread. For individuals, it can lead to serious financial problems, as they might accumulate debt and struggle to save money for important things. This overspending also causes stress and unhappiness in the long term. From a larger perspective, constant consumption creates a lot of waste, which is bad for the environment. More products mean more manufacturing, which uses natural resources and increases pollution. Ultimately, this cycle of buying and discarding puts a strain on our planet.\n\nIn conclusion, the habit of buying unneeded products is driven by advertising, social trends, and easy credit. This behaviour results in personal financial difficulties and severe environmental damage.",
    "high": "It is increasingly common for individuals in modern society to acquire goods and services they do not genuinely require. This essay will examine the primary causes of this pervasive behaviour and discuss its various effects on individuals, the environment, and society at large.\n\nOne significant cause of this trend is the sophisticated marketing strategies employed by companies, which are adept at creating artificial demand and influencing consumer desires. Pervasive advertising, particularly through social media, often makes aspirational spending seem necessary. Furthermore, increasing disposable income in many developed nations allows for greater discretionary spending, making impulse purchases more frequent. The ease of online shopping and readily available credit facilities also contribute, as immediate gratification often overrides careful financial consideration.\n\nThe effects of this overconsumption are far-reaching. Individually, it often leads to financial strain and mounting personal debt, diminishing savings and overall economic stability. Environmentally, the continuous demand for new products accelerates resource depletion and generates vast amounts of waste, contributing significantly to a larger carbon footprint and ecological degradation. Societally, this trend fosters a culture of materialism where possessions are equated with happiness and status, potentially leading to dissatisfaction and a focus on short-term gratification rather than sustainable well-being.\n\nIn conclusion, the acquisition of unnecessary products is largely driven by persuasive marketing and increased economic capacity. Its repercussions range from individual financial insecurity to severe environmental consequences and a pervasive shift towards a consumerist culture."
  },
  "25f3b1ba-917e-4b1b-a083-20d132c937b3": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Tourism is a big thing for many places. It can be a good thing but also a bad thing for the people who live there. It has both positive and negative impacts on the local community.\n\nWhen tourist come, they spend money. This money helps the local businesses. For example, restaurant get more customer, and shop sell more things. Local people can find job in hotel or driving car for the visitor. This make the economy good for the community. The local area can develop new facilities because of this money from the visitors.\n\nBut tourism also have problem. Many tourist can make the place very crowded, especially in popular area. Sometime, the price of food and house become more expensive for the local resident because of the tourist demand. It is difficult for local to afford living. Also, too much visitor can damage the nature and historical building if they not careful. This can change the community and sometimes destroy the local culture or quiet life.\n\nIn conclusion, tourism bring money and jobs to local community. But it also bring problem like crowded place and higher cost for the people. It has both good and bad side for the place where tourist visit.",
    "mid": "Tourism certainly has both positive and negative effects on the people who live in a particular region. It can bring many good things, but also some difficulties for the residents and their environment.\n\nOn the one hand, holidaymakers usually bring a lot of income to the local community. These visitors spend their cash in hotels, restaurants, and shops, which helps to create jobs for local people. For example, young people can find work as guides or in hospitality. This financial gain can improve the overall economy of the area, allowing for better public services and facilities for everyone living there.\n\nHowever, there are also significant downsides. The presence of many travellers can lead to overcrowding, especially in popular destinations. This often results in more rubbish and pollution, which damages the natural environment. Beautiful beaches or historical sites can suffer from too many human presence. Furthermore, an increase in people visiting can push up prices for housing and basic goods, making it harder for local citizens to afford to live in their own towns. Sometimes, the original culture of a place can change to please the foreign visitors.\n\nIn conclusion, while the economic benefits from people coming to see a region are clear, the challenges to the environment and the local way of life must be considered carefully. It is important to find a good balance between attracting visitors and protecting the local community.",
    "high": "Global travel has become increasingly accessible, leading to significant interactions between visitors and host populations. While this burgeoning industry undoubtedly brings numerous advantages, it also poses considerable challenges to the areas it explores. This essay will discuss both the positive and negative effects of such interactions.\n\nOne primary benefit of attracting holidaymakers is the economic upliftment it brings to a destination. The influx of revenue from these guests can stimulate local businesses, creating employment opportunities in hospitality, retail, and transportation sectors. Furthermore, increased earnings often lead to improved infrastructure, such as better roads and utilities, benefiting residents directly. Moreover, cultural exchange can occur, broadening perspectives on both sides.\n\nHowever, the presence of numerous sightseers can exert considerable pressure on local environments and cultures. Fragile ecosystems, for instance, may suffer from pollution and overuse. Additionally, the constant exposure to foreign customs can gradually erode traditional practices, particularly in remote regions. The elevated demand for services and goods driven by vacationers also often causes property prices and daily expenses to soar, making life unaffordable for native inhabitants.\n\nIn conclusion, while the visitor industry undeniably injects financial resources and fosters development in various locales, its potential for environmental degradation and cultural disruption is equally significant. Therefore, effective management strategies are crucial to ensure that these leisure pursuits contribute sustainably to their chosen destinations."
  },
  "b0be2fd1-0b50-479a-9b10-ef95e8a5d503": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "It is true that economic growth often bring problems for the environment. I agree with this statement very much. When countries want develop, they do many things that make nature suffer.\n\nFor instance, many factory are build for make more product. These factory release bad smoke into the air and also chemicals to the water. This cause air pollution and water pollution. Many people get sick from this and also fish die in river. Also, for economic growth, more cars is on the road. Car make too much smoke too. This all make planet very dirty.\n\nAnother point is when country want develop, they need many material. So, they cut down the forest for wood or for make land. Forest is home for animal, so animal lose their home. And trees is very important for clean air, but when many trees cut, air become less clean. This mean nature is destroy for money.\n\nIn conclusion, I think economic growth have a big cost to our environment. We must find a way for make money but not make environment bad. Government and people need to think for this seriously.",
    "mid": "It is clear that economic growth usually has a negative impact on the environment. I totally agree with this idea because when countries try to make more money, they often forget about our planet and use up many resources.\n\nFor example, many factories are built to produce more goods and jobs. These factories make a lot of pollution, like dirty air from chimneys and dirty water from chemicals going into rivers. Also, to build new cities or grow food, people cut down many trees, which is called deforestation. This is bad for animals and also makes the air worse for everyone to breathe because trees help to clean it. We also use a lot of fuel and minerals from the earth which will soon run out.\n\nSome people think that new technology can help us have economic growth without hurting the environment. They talk about clean energy like solar or wind power. However, even these things need resources to build them, and they are not always enough to support all the growth that countries want. It is very difficult to find a perfect balance.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly believe that chasing economic growth often comes with a big price for the environment. Governments and businesses need to think more about how their plans affect our world for the future.",
    "high": "It is widely observed that the relentless pursuit of economic prosperity frequently entails significant environmental consequences. I largely agree with this assertion, as historical and contemporary examples underscore this challenging dynamic.\n\nHistorically, industrialisation has been built upon the exploitation of natural resources. Unfettered economic expansion often necessitates extensive deforestation for agriculture or urbanisation, leading to widespread habitat degradation. Furthermore, increased manufacturing and transportation fuel a substantial carbon footprint, exacerbating climate change and air pollution. The extraction of finite resources, such as fossil fuels and minerals, also places an unsustainable burden on ecosystems, threatening biodiversity and natural resilience. This short-sighted approach prioritises immediate fiscal gains at the expense of long-term ecological health.\n\nHowever, it is crucial to recognise that this correlation is not immutable. With discerning policies and technological advancements, the environmental impact can be mitigated. Investing in renewable energy, promoting circular economies, and implementing stricter environmental regulations can foster 'green growth'. Some nations are demonstrating that sustainable practices, driven by innovation and a commitment to ecological stewardship, can indeed lead to economic prosperity without detrimental ecological footprints. The challenge lies in striking a balance and shifting away from traditional growth paradigms.\n\nIn conclusion, while economic development has historically imposed substantial environmental costs, particularly through resource depletion and pollution, a future where growth is decoupled from environmental harm is achievable. It requires a concerted global effort, strategic investments, and a fundamental reassessment of our intrinsic values."
  },
  "6a7b6e14-84f7-4633-b49e-ab6df66bc3c6": {
    "kind": "WRITING",
    "low": "Globalisation is big thing in world now. It make many country connect. Some people say this make our traditions go away. I think this is true for many part of world.\n\nFor example, many young people now like to listen to music from other country. They not listen to traditional music from their own place. Also, food is change. In my country, we have own food. But now, many people like fast food like burgers or pizza from West country. This is not our culture food. Also, traditional clothes is not popular. Many people like to wear modern clothes like from America or Europe. So, this make old traditions less important for young generation.\n\nAnother point is media. We can watch films and TV shows from everywhere. This mean people see other way of life and they think it is good. They want to be like that. So, they forget about their own special culture things. Old people try to keep traditions but young people are interested in new things because of globalisation effect.\n\nIn conclusion, I think globalisation really make cultural traditions lost. Many old ways of life are not practice anymore because everyone want to be same.",
    "mid": "Many people think that cultures are changing because of globalisation. This is a big problem for traditional ways of life in different countries.\n\nOne reason for this is that global companies bring their products everywhere. For example, fast food like McDonald's is now in many cities, and young people prefer it instead of local foods. This means old recipes are not used so much. Also, Western music and films are very popular, so young generations might not learn about their own country's songs or stories. This is how traditions slowly disappear.\n\nAlso, when people travel more or work in other countries, they often adapt to new customs. Sometimes, they forget their own traditional clothes or festivals when they live far away. The internet also shows many different cultures, which is good, but it can make local traditions seem less important to young peoples. So, globalisation makes the world similar, and local uniqueness is lost.\n\nIn conclusion, it is clear that globalisation is having a strong effect on cultural traditions. It makes them weaker because of new products, entertainment, and people moving around. It is important to remember our traditions, but it is hard with a global world.",
    "high": "The assertion that cultural traditions are being eroded by globalisation is a widely debated topic, encompassing both profound losses and surprising adaptations. While it is undeniable that certain indigenous practices face immense pressure, globalisation also acts as a catalyst for cultural reinterpretation and dissemination.\n\nIndeed, the pervasive influence of global media and rampant consumerism often leads to cultural homogenisation. Younger generations, particularly, may gravitate towards internationally popular trends, thereby marginalising ancestral languages, traditional attire, and unique culinary practices. The dominance of a few global languages, for instance, imperils countless smaller linguistic traditions, risking the loss of their embedded knowledge and distinctive worldview. This cultural dilution can diminish the intricate tapestry of human diversity.\n\nHowever, globalisation is not solely an agent of erosion; it can also be a powerful tool for cultural preservation and revitalisation. Digital platforms and increased international travel facilitate the widespread dissemination of traditional arts, music, and festivals, allowing them to reach new audiences and even garner renewed local interest. Furthermore, the very interaction between cultures can foster hybrid forms, creating innovative expressions that blend ancient roots with contemporary influences. This cross-cultural fertilisation demonstrates a dynamic evolution rather than outright disappearance.\n\nUltimately, while some aspects of heritage undoubtedly fade under global pressures, many others transform or find new avenues for expression. The narrative is therefore complex, highlighting both the vulnerability and the remarkable resilience of human culture in an interconnected world."
  },
  "81138d47-db1b-4802-9cdb-8adca7ee275f": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Last weekend, I was visiting my friend. Her name is Ana. On Saturday morning, I was going to her house. I took the bus. The bus was very full with peoples. When I arrived, Ana was already there. She was waited for me outside her door. We went inside. We was talking about our week. Then, she cooking lunch for us. It was a simple food, rice and chicken. While we were eating, we was laughing much. After lunch, we watch a movie together. It was very funny movie. I was enjoyed it. Around 4 o'clock, I say goodbye and I was went home. It was a good day, I was happy to see Ana.",
    "mid": "Uh, last weekend, um, I went to a park. I went with my friend, uh, Anya. We were, uh, planning to have a picnic there. When we arrived, many peoples were there, already. We walked around for a bit, looking for a good place. We found a nice spot near a big tree. While we were sitting down, the sun was shining very brightly. We were eating our sandwiches and drinking some juice. It was very relaxing. Then, uh, suddenly, a small dog ran past us. It was chasing a ball, very fast. We laughed a little bit. We stayed for maybe two hours, just, uh, talking and enjoying the weather. After that, we packed up our things and we walked home. It was a very good day, yeah.",
    "high": "Last weekend was a really good mix of activity and relaxation. On Saturday morning, I was planning some errands, but my friend called while I was having breakfast, suggesting we go cycling. So, we spontaneously decided to head to the park. We rode our bikes for a couple of hours, enjoying the sunny weather. It was quite refreshing.\n\nOn Sunday, the weather was cooler and rainy, so I chose a relaxed day indoors. I was just reading a novel when my flatmate came home. We decided to watch a film together, an interesting documentary about wildlife, which was quite captivating. Afterwards, I was just tidying up my room. It was a pretty peaceful and enjoyable weekend overall."
  },
  "bb30d000-a571-4ba1-92f3-7a3735dbd8d2": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Next year, umm, my plan... I going to study more English. Is very important for me. So, I going to go English class, maybe three time a week. My English is not good, so I need to improve.\n\nAnd, umm, also, I want find new job. My current job is not good pay. So, I will looking for better job. Maybe in a shop or a cafe. This job is more money.\n\nAlso, my sister... she living far from me. So I going to visit her. Maybe in July or August. We are going to cook food together. My friend, we are meeting for football sometimes. So, next year, busy. Very busy year for me. Yes.",
    "mid": "Well, next year, I have many plans. Firstly, I am going to study English more harder. I think is very important for my future job. Also, I am planning to search for a new job, probably in a bigger company. My friend works there, so I will ask him about. I will try to get a better salary, you know. My family and I are going to visit my uncle in another city. We are planning travel by train. It will be nice holiday. And I am going to start jogging every morning. I want to be more fit. So, I hope next year will be a good year for me.",
    "high": "Well, next year, I'm going to be finishing my Master's degree, which is my primary focus right now. My graduation is set for June, so I'm putting a lot of effort into my final thesis. After that, I'm planning to actively search for a job in my field, specifically data analysis. I'm already looking at different companies and I'm going to start applying for positions around March or April.\n\nBeyond my career, I'm hoping to travel a bit. I'm arranging a trip with some friends to visit Japan for two weeks in July, right after I graduate. We're going to explore Tokyo and Kyoto. I think it will be a fantastic experience. Hopefully, I will have secured a job by then, but either way, I'm really looking forward to a year full of new challenges and exciting adventures."
  },
  "dfbf1016-54b8-48c0-8eb7-a117b768f44d": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "One trip I remember good... it was to the beach. My family and me, we went there last year, in summer. We was travelling by car for about three hours. It was long drive.\n\nWhen we arrived, the sun was very bright and many people was already there. My father, he booked a small room in a hotel. We were staying near the beach. I remember, before this trip, I never see the big sea like that. So it was new for me.\n\nWe was swimming every day and also my brother and I was building a sandcastle. One day, it was raining when we was eating breakfast, but then it stopped. The food was delicious. I like the fish they cook. It was a very memorable trip for me because it was first time I go to the sea with my family.",
    "mid": "I want to describe a memorable trip I took, last year. It was a trip to the beach, a very nice beach called, Maya Bay. We went there with my family. Before we went, my sister had seen many pictures of this beach on the internet, and she was really excited about it.\n\nWhen we arrived, the sun was shining very brightly, and many people were already swimming in the sea. The water looked so clear and blue. We stayed for three days. Every day, we were enjoying the sun and also tried local food. I remember one evening, we ate fresh seafood at a small restaurant, and it was so delicious. I had never tasted seafood so fresh before. It was a very good experience and I was very happy.",
    "high": "Okay, so I’d like to describe a truly memorable trip I took last year to Patagonia, specifically Torres del Paine National Park in Chile. I had always wanted to visit that region, so I finally booked a solo adventure.\n\nWhen I arrived, the weather was quite unpredictable; it was raining heavily for the first couple of days, disappointing me as I had been dreaming of clear views. However, as I was trekking towards the 'W' circuit's first viewpoint, the clouds suddenly cleared.\n\nThe sun was beginning to set, and the famous granite towers, the Torres, just appeared, bathed in golden light. It was absolutely breathtaking. We had been hiking for hours, and seeing that incredible panorama made all the effort worthwhile, an unforgettable experience I’ll cherish forever."
  },
  "07f298df-3835-4c47-814d-d9408aa5f924": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, uhm, if I, if I grew up in another country... for example, like, if I had grow up in, uh, Canada. My life, it would be very different, I think.\n\nFirst, my language. I think I would speak English more, very good. And my first language, maybe I would not speak it much now. So, if my parents had moved there when I was small, I think I would have gone to Canadian school. And my friends, they, they would be from Canada. So maybe my sports, my hobbies, everything, it would be different. If I play ice hockey in Canada, I would be a good player, maybe. And the food, if I eat there, I think I would eat different food always. So, if my parents had not moved from my country, I would not know this country now. It's... it's very different. My whole life.",
    "mid": "If I had grown up in another country, like Canada, my life would be very different. If I had lived there, my first language would be English, so I will have a Canadian accent. My education would have been completely different. I would go to a public school there, not a private one. If I had gone to Canada school, I would have had more chance to learn sports like ice hockey. I would be more active.\n\nAlso, if I had grown up in Canada, my friends would be from many places. I would have more open mind. If I would have a different friend group, my interests is different. I would choose a different career. My personality is probably not the same. My life would be another story.",
    "high": "If I had grown up in a country like Japan, my life would undoubtedly be profoundly different. For a start, I would have learned Japanese as my mother tongue, and my proficiency in English probably wouldn't be as strong today. I believe my personality would also have been shaped by their culture; for example, I might have developed a much greater emphasis on collective harmony rather than individual expression. If I had attended school there, my academic priorities would have certainly shifted, perhaps towards science or technology, given Japan's strengths in those areas. Consequently, if I were living there now, my entire social circle and even my career path would be completely unrecognisable from my current reality. I think I would also have a completely different appreciation for seasons and nature, which is a big part of Japanese culture."
  },
  "8043768c-641c-4d8e-a995-2fcfc009b650": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "When I was child, we don't have many technology. Only TV, and maybe a landline phone for talk. It was very simple life. Today, it is totally different. Children have had mobiles since they are young. They have use internet for games and social media. Technology has change very fast, it's more faster now than before. My childhood was less connected.\n\nFor future, I think technology will probably be more everywhere. Maybe robots will do many jobs in house. Children might learn with virtual reality, not only books. People could have flying cars in the city. It is going to be very different life, more automatic and smart.",
    "mid": "When I was a child, technology was much simpler. We mostly had a landline phone, and computers were only for school. We just watched TV, no streaming.\n\nToday, it's very different. Technology has really changed everything. Most people have smartphones, which are much more powerful. We use the internet for everything – learning, communication, fun. Social media has become very common; my parents have even started using apps.\n\nFor the future, I think technology might become more integrated. We could see more smart homes. AI will be much smarter, assisting us daily. It may be we just talk to devices. I guess it will be easier, but also a bit scary.",
    "high": "When I was a child, technology was certainly much simpler and far less integrated into daily life. We mostly had a bulky desktop computer for occasional games, and landline phones were still more common than mobiles.\n\nToday, the contrast is incredible. Smartphones have completely transformed communication and information access, something my childhood self couldn't have imagined. Technology has become significantly more pervasive, from smart home devices to constant online connectivity. We've seen a massive shift towards mobile platforms and ubiquitous internet.\n\nLooking ahead, I believe technology will become even more seamless. We might see less reliance on physical devices as augmented reality or brain-computer interfaces could become more mainstream. AI will likely play a much bigger role in automating tasks, making our lives potentially easier, but perhaps also raising new concerns about privacy. It’s truly an exciting, if slightly daunting, prospect."
  },
  "bc2efa6a-6d30-40f1-8c11-d9d774d62d85": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I think my regret... It was one time, I don't study for my math exam. This was in high school. I remember I felt very, very bad after the results. I was too lazy, you know? I just play computer games all the time. So, I not prepared. I fail it. My teacher was angry. My parents too, they yell at me. I think... if I just study little bit more, I would pass.\n\nWhat I would do different? I think I should have... no, I *would* open my books. I would ask my friend, he is good at math, for help. If I did that, I will not fail. Yes. It was a big lesson for me. Now, if I have important things, I try to prepare good. This regret really help me learn.",
    "mid": "Okay, so, um, a decision I regret. I think it's about my university studies. When I was, uh, finished high school, I chose a course, you know, because my friends also choose it. It was, um, engineering. But after one year, I realized it was not really for me. I was not interested, and it was very difficult. So I feel quite regret about this choice.\n\nIf I had more time to think, I would choosing a different subject, maybe something like marketing or business. Because if I was more careful then, I wouldn't have wasted so much time and money. I should have research more about different careers, not just follow my friends. And I could have talk to more people who already study that course. If I had done that, my situation now would be much better, I think. Because now is hard to find a job in engineering what I study.",
    "high": "Well, one decision I really regret is turning down a job offer straight after university. I'd received a fantastic proposal from a reputable company, but I was so keen on taking a gap year and travelling. If I had been a bit more forward-thinking then, I definitely would have accepted it without hesitation.\n\nI remember thinking, 'Oh, I can always travel later,' but if I were in that situation now, I would certainly have made a different choice. I spent almost a year struggling to find a job in the same field, which was quite disheartening. I should have considered the long-term implications more carefully. If I could turn back time, I would certainly take that initial offer because it would have provided a much smoother start to my career, and I could have saved up more money for travel afterwards. It was a valuable lesson in career planning."
  },
  "bc392be6-6d47-4ff0-8021-f9d6acca518c": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "My... my favourite hobby... is listen to music. Yes. I like listen music very much. Uhm... I do this... every day. Uhm... when I feel... tired, or happy, I listen music. I have a... a phone, and I have earphone. I put earphone in my ear and... listen songs. My favourite music is... pop music. I like... fast beat. Yes. Uhm... I also like... sometimes... slow music. When I am... sad. I like to hear... the singers... voice. Uhm... it make me feel... better. Yes. I listen many type of songs. Sometime... I listen... on the bus, sometime I listen... at home. It is... my... my best thing to do. Yes.",
    "mid": "My favourite hobby is... uh... reading book. I really enjoy reading because it make me feel relax after a long day at work. I usually read at night, before I go to bed. I like many different types of books, like a fiction story or sometimes non-fiction.\n\nWhen I read, I just open the book and start to read pages. It is very good to follow the story and learn about new character. Sometimes, I go to library to borrowing book, it is cheaper than buying new one. I think reading improve my vocabulary and my knowledge. I like to imagine what happen in the book. It is very calm activity for me.",
    "high": "My favourite hobby is definitely cooking. I find it incredibly therapeutic, especially after a long day, as it's a creative process where I can experiment with various ingredients and *flavour profiles*. I particularly enjoy exploring international *cuisines*, trying my hand at a spicy Thai curry or baking a delicate French pastry.\n\nWhen in the kitchen, I appreciate the entire journey – from meticulously *chopping* vegetables and *sautéing* aromatics, to carefully *seasoning* and plating the final dish. The aroma of garlic *sautéing* or fresh bread *baking* is truly comforting, and the satisfaction of creating something delicious from scratch is immense. It helps me unwind and focus away from daily stresses."
  },
  "53738823-5f82-41b0-94d8-0f73871ba218": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I like a sunny day, very much. When the sun is shine, I feel very happy. Uh, I can go outside, like, to the park or just walk. It is good for my mood. I also like when is little warm, not too hot.\n\nBut I don't like when it is rain. Rain make me feel very sad. I must stay inside my house, and I cannot do many thing. And also, I don't like very cold weather. When it is very cold, my body feel bad, and I don't like the strong wind also. I think, uh, hot summer days is also not so good sometimes.",
    "mid": "Okay, so, um, I like very much the sunny weather. Yes. When sun is out, I feel very happy. Is good because you can go outside, maybe to a park or for walk. I like also when is warm, but no too hot. Just nice. It make me feel like, uh, energetic and good.\n\nBut, uh, I not like very much the rain weather. When it's rain, is very difficult to do anything, and I feel a little bit sad or bored. Especially if it's cold rain, then it's more bad. I also don't like when is very windy, because everything, like, it blow around, and is not comfortable. Stormy weather, with thunder and lightning, is also not my favorite. It make me feel a little bit afraid, you know.",
    "high": "I definitely have strong preferences when it comes to weather. I absolutely adore sunny, warm days, especially when there's a gentle, refreshing breeze. It's just so invigorating to feel the glorious sunshine on your skin and see clear skies. Those days make me feel incredibly uplifted and energetic, perfect for being outdoors, maybe going for a walk or just enjoying a picnic. It truly brightens my mood.\n\nOn the flip side, I really dislike anything too extreme. Heavy, torrential rain for days on end can be quite depressing, making everything feel gloomy and damp. And I particularly dislike biting cold weather with strong, gale-force winds. It just makes me feel miserable and constantly confined indoors. Thunderstorms can also be quite unsettling, especially when they're very intense. I much prefer the predictability and comfort of milder, sunnier conditions."
  },
  "eae633a6-687e-47e5-9d41-7c5e3457a4d7": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, I want to talk about my mother. I admire her very much. She is a very good person. She always work so hard, every day. She wake up early and make breakfast for all family. She is very kind. She like to help others, always. She have big heart, I think. She always happy, even when problem happen. My mother is, uh, very positive. She teach me many things, like study hard and be honest. She never get angry with me. She always support me, you know. I think she is strong woman. For me, she is best person. Yes, I admire my mother so much.",
    "mid": "Okay, so I want to talk about my mother. She is a person I really admire. She is a very hard-working person. She always wake up early and go to work, and then she come home and do all the housework for our family. She never complain, you know?\n\nAlso, she is very kind. She always help people when they need help, like she often help her neighbours with their kids or cooking. She is also very patient with me and my siblings, even when we are difficult. She always encouraging us to do our best. I think she is very down to earth too; she doesn't like fancy things and she is happy with simple life. So, yes, she is my biggest inspiration.",
    "high": "Okay, the person I admire most is definitely my mother. She's truly an incredible individual. What I appreciate most about her is her unwavering determination and sheer hard work. She's always been incredibly diligent, balancing a demanding career with raising a family, and she approached everything with such dedication.\n\nShe's also very kind-hearted and incredibly supportive. Whenever someone is in need, she's the first to offer help, showing real empathy and understanding. She has a very down-to-earth perspective on life, which I find incredibly refreshing; she never gets caught up in superficial things. Despite facing various challenges throughout her life, she always maintains such a positive outlook and an amazing resilience. I really look up to her for that – she’s a wonderful role model for me."
  },
  "1f1fbcac-e7a8-4895-a059-b249b7a59f96": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Okay, social media is important for me. Every day I look my phone. I have Facebook, WhatsApp. I use this for connect my friends. My friends, they live in different city. So, I can see their photos, their post. It is good for keep in touch, you know?\n\nAlso, sometimes I see news. Like, what is happen in the world. But not too much. Mostly I like to see friends. I spend maybe one hour in the evening. Sometimes more. I upload my photo sometimes, but not very often. My family also use WhatsApp to send message. So, it is useful for me to contact many peoples. It is part of my daily life, yes. I like it. My phone is always with me.",
    "mid": "Okay, so social media is, like, very important in my daily life. I use it, uh, every day, you know? Like, when I wake up, I usually check my phone, uh, to see new messages. My main platforms are WhatsApp and Instagram. For WhatsApp, it's very good to, uh, communicate with my family who live far away, like, my cousins in another city. We can send quick messages and photos.\n\nAnd Instagram, I like to scroll through, uh, friends' posts and sometimes watch, uh, short videos. It's a way to keep updated about what my friends are doing, you know? Sometimes I also use Facebook, but not so much now. It helps me to pass the time when I am bored, but also, it can waste time sometimes. So, yes, it's a big part of my day.",
    "high": "Social media undoubtedly plays a pretty pivotal role in my daily life, I'd say. Primarily, it's about staying connected. I use platforms like Instagram and WhatsApp extensively to keep in touch with friends and family, especially those living abroad. It really facilitates instant messaging and sharing updates, making *long-distance communication* much more manageable and spontaneous.\n\nBeyond personal connections, I find it invaluable for *information dissemination*. My news feeds often provide *real-time updates* on current events or *breaking news*, curated to my interests. I also follow various *online communities* related to my hobbies, which allows for *digital interaction* and learning from others globally. While I try to be *mindful of my screen time*, its utility for both *social interaction* and *accessing diverse content* is undeniable, making it an *indispensable tool* in my routine."
  },
  "e62b8699-7c14-4f3d-b670-ea3131390d2b": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "I think... umm... money... is... important. Yes. Because, uhh, people need money for buy food. And for, umm, rent home. Or, uh, pay bills. So, yes, money can make you happy for these things.\n\nBut... I think... not only money make happy. You know? Because, umm, if you have much money, but you are alone. No friends. No family. Is not happy. So, happy is, uh, also about... umm... friends, and family, and, umm... love. Yes. These things is no money buy. So, money is good, but is not everything for happiness. Some things... is more important. Yeah.",
    "mid": "Okay, so, money and happiness. I think money, it cannot buy real happiness, you know? Like, you can buy nice things, a big house, a fast car, and these things, they make you feel good for a little time. But this is not real happiness, I think. Real happiness is more about like, your family, your friends, and if you are healthy.\n\nMoney can help, yes, because if you don't have money, you have many problems. Like, you cannot pay your rent or buy food. So, having money can make life easier, and this can make you feel less stress. But it cannot buy love or make you happy inside, you know? True happiness is, um, from inside you. So, in my opinion, it's good to have money, but it's not everything for happiness.",
    "high": "It's undeniably a nuanced topic. While money alone cannot directly purchase happiness, it certainly plays a significant role in facilitating it. Sufficient funds alleviate considerable stress, enabling individuals to meet basic needs, access quality healthcare, and enjoy enriching experiences. This financial security provides crucial peace of mind, foundational for well-being.\n\nHowever, I believe there's a point of diminishing returns. Beyond fundamental requirements, wealth's correlation with genuine contentment weakens. Money buys luxuries, but it doesn't guarantee strong relationships, purpose, or intrinsic joy. In fact, an excessive pursuit can be a double-edged sword, leading to new anxieties. Ultimately, true happiness is contingent on far more profound, non-material aspects of life."
  },
  "44941dec-0c56-4a8d-9f52-1d8d0428fcf5": {
    "kind": "SPEAKING",
    "low": "Uh, one global issue, I feel very strong about, uh, is pollution. Yes. I think pollution is a big problem everywhere now. Like, uh, in the air, the water, and, uh, on the land too. You know?\n\nI feel strong because it's, uh, very bad for people. Many people get sick because they breathe bad air, especially in big cities. And, uh, also for animals, like fish. Many fish die from plastic in the ocean. Uh, it's very sad.\n\nThe impact is, uh, the earth gets more and more dirty. Future generation will not have clean water or clean air. They will have big problem. We must, uh, stop it, but it's difficult because, uh, factories make smoke and people, uh, throw much rubbish everywhere. Yes. It's a very big issue.",
    "mid": "Okay, uhm, I want to talk about global warming. I think it is a very big global issue, uh, affect everyone, you know, around the world. I feel very strong about it because, uh, it's impacting so many things already.\n\nFor example, the temperature is getting higher and higher each year. This make the ice in the polar regions, uhm, melting. So, the sea levels, they are rise, and, uh, many cities near the ocean will have flood in the future. Also, we are seeing many, uh, strange weather patterns. Like, some places have very, very hot summer, or too much rain, or big storms. This is bad for farming and people's homes.\n\nIt also impacts, uhm, animals. Many animals lose their natural home because of this. So, it's a very serious problem for our environment and also for our future generation life. We must to do something for it.",
    "high": "Okay, a global issue I feel particularly strongly about is definitely climate change, or more broadly, environmental degradation. It's an urgent problem that truly affects every corner of our planet. The impact is incredibly far-reaching and multifaceted. We're already witnessing more frequent and intense extreme weather phenomena, from devastating droughts and wildfires to unprecedented floods. These events not only cause immense damage to infrastructure but also lead to significant loss of life and livelihoods. Beyond the immediate destruction, there's the long-term threat to global food security, as agricultural systems struggle to adapt to unpredictable weather patterns. Furthermore, the rising sea levels are displacing coastal communities and threatening vital ecosystems. It also severely impacts biodiversity, pushing countless species towards extinction, which in turn disrupts ecological balances essential for human well-being. Ultimately, it's a crisis that demands immediate collective action to mitigate its escalating consequences on human societies and the natural world."
  }
}